1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:05,100
Chapter 16: Genetics.

3
00:00:05,100 --> 00:00:27,450
Genomics In Obstetrics Abnormalities Of Chromosome Number Abnormalities Of Chromosome Structure Mosaicism Modes Of Inheritance Cytogenetic Analysis Chromosomal Microarray Analysis Whole Genome And Whole Exome Sequencing Fetal Dna In The Maternal Circulation References Genetics is the study of genes, heredity, and the variation of inherited characteristics.

4
00:00:27,450 --> 00:00:37,412
Medical genetics addresses the etiology and pathogenesis of human diseases that are at least partially genetic in origin, as well as their prediction and prevention.

5
00:00:37,412 --> 00:00:50,200
Whereas a gene is a specific sequence of deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) on a single chromosome that codes for a particular protein, a genome is the entirety of all genes that make up an organism.

6
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:55,037
Genomics is the study of how genes function and interact with one other.

7
00:00:55,037 --> 00:01:01,175
Chromosomal, mendelian, and nonmendelian genetic conditions are reviewed in this chapter.

8
00:01:01,175 --> 00:01:09,787
Prenatal and preimplantation genetic testing and newborn genetic screening are discussed in Chapters 17 and 32, respectively.

9
00:01:09,787 --> 00:01:12,212
Genetic disease is common.

10
00:01:12,212 --> 00:01:28,700
Two to 3 percent of newborns have a recognized structural abnormality, another 3 percent are diagnosed with an abnormality by age 5 years, and yet another 8 to 10 percent are found to have a functional or developmental abnormality before reaching adulthood.

11
00:01:28,700 --> 00:01:31,150
Genomics In Obstetrics.

12
00:01:31,150 --> 00:01:40,987
The Human Genome Project was an international research program that sequenced the 3 billion base pairs and more than 20,000 genes that make up the human genome.

13
00:01:40,987 --> 00:01:48,600
It paved the way for research into gene organization and function in an effort to understand the molecular basis of disease.

14
00:01:48,600 --> 00:01:52,737
More than 99 percent of our DNA is identical.

15
00:01:52,737 --> 00:01:59,300
The coding regions of DNA—exons— constitute only 1.5 percent of the genome.

16
00:01:59,300 --> 00:02:04,262
An exome is the entirety of all the exons that an organism contains.

17
00:02:04,262 --> 00:02:11,012
Introns are DNA sequences involved in coding regulation and make up 24 percent of the genome.

18
00:02:11,012 --> 00:02:14,437
Intergenic DNA composes the remainder.

19
00:02:14,437 --> 00:02:22,987
Our genetic code varies once every 200 to 500 base pairs, usually as a single-nucleotide polymorphism (SNP).

20
00:02:22,987 --> 00:02:34,362
Thus, whole genome sequencing and whole exome sequencing, described later (p. 327), hold tremendous potential to further elucidate genetic variants in disease.

21
00:02:34,362 --> 00:02:43,175
Toward this goal, genetic and genomic databases are maintained by the National Center for Biotechnology Information (2021).

22
00:02:43,175 --> 00:02:50,712
These are freely accessible and can be indispensable to providers who offer counseling and testing for genetic conditions.

23
00:02:50,712 --> 00:03:01,200
The GeneReviews database has in-depth clinical information for more than 800 genetic conditions, including diagnostic criteria and management considerations.

24
00:03:01,200 --> 00:03:14,325
The Genetic Testing Registry (GTR) database contains information for nearly 80,000 genetic tests and instructions for specimen collection and transport to individual laboratories throughout the world .

25
00:03:14,325 --> 00:03:25,437
The National Library of Medicine (2021) has also established a genetic information database, the MedlinePlus Genetics, which is intended for the lay population.

26
00:03:25,437 --> 00:03:35,850
It provides explanations of more than 1300 genetic conditions, a glossary of genetic concepts, and links to more comprehensive medical resources.

27
00:03:35,850 --> 00:03:38,512
Chromosomal Abnormalities.

28
00:03:38,512 --> 00:03:43,175
Chromosomal abnormalities figure prominently in genetic disease.

29
00:03:43,175 --> 00:04:01,150
They account for >50 percent of first-trimester miscarriages, approximately 20 percent of second-trimester losses, and 6 to 8 percent of stillbirths and earlychildhood deaths (Reddy, 2012; Stevenson, 2004; Wou, 2016).

30
00:04:01,150 --> 00:04:10,625
Based on data from population-based registries, chromosomal abnormalities are identified in 0.4 percent of recognized pregnancies .

31
00:04:10,625 --> 00:04:13,237
Most of these are aneuploidies.

32
00:04:13,237 --> 00:04:23,200
Trisomy 21 accounts for >50 percent, trisomy 18 for nearly 15 percent, and trisomy 13 for 5 percent of cases .

33
00:04:23,200 --> 00:04:40,987
If no karyotypic abnormality is identified in a fetus with a structural anomaly, additional testing with chromosomal microarray analysis is anticipated to detect a chromosomal deletion or duplication—a copy number variant—in approximately 6.5 percent of cases .

34
00:04:40,987 --> 00:04:43,412
Figure 16-1.

35
00:04:43,412 --> 00:04:47,500
Prevalence and relative proportion of selected chromosomal

36
00:04:47,668 --> 00:05:03,255
abnormalities from EUROCAT (European Surveillance of Congenital Anomalies) population-based registries that included >10,000 aneuploid live births, fetal deaths, and pregnancy terminations, 2000–2006.

37
00:05:03,205 --> 00:05:06,018
# Standard Nomenclature.

38
00:05:06,018 --> 00:05:13,168
Chromosomal abnormalities are described using the International System for Human Cytogenomic Nomenclature .

39
00:05:13,168 --> 00:05:24,893
Abnormalities fall into two broad categories: abnormal chromosome number, such as trisomy, and abnormal chromosome structure, such as a DNA segment deletion or duplication.

40
00:05:24,893 --> 00:05:34,830
Each chromosome has a short arm, termed the “p” or petit arm, and a long arm, known as the “q” arm, selected because it is the next letter in the alphabet.

41
00:05:34,830 --> 00:05:38,193
The two arms are separated by the centromere.

42
00:05:38,193 --> 00:05:45,905
When reporting a karyotype, the total number of chromosomes is listed first, corresponding to the number of centromeres.

43
00:05:45,905 --> 00:05:53,568
This is followed by the sex chromosomes, XX or XY, and then by a description of any structural variation.

44
00:05:53,568 --> 00:06:04,230
Specific chromosomal abnormalities are indicated by standard abbreviations, such as del (deletion), inv (inversion), and t (translocation).

45
00:06:04,230 --> 00:06:10,918
The affected region or bands of the arms are then reported, so that the reader will know the exact location.

46
00:06:10,918 --> 00:06:14,493
Examples are shown in Table 16-1.

47
00:06:14,493 --> 00:06:16,880
Table 16-1.

48
00:06:16,880 --> 00:06:35,205
Examples of Karyotype Designations Using the 2020 International System for Human Cytogenomic Nomenclature Nomenclature is similar for fluorescence in situ hybridization (FISH), a technique used to rapidly identify a specific chromosome abnormality (p. 325).

49
00:06:35,205 --> 00:06:43,580
The abbreviation ish applies when in situ hybridization is performed on metaphase cells and nuc ish when performed on interphase nuclei.

50
00:06:43,580 --> 00:06:57,080
If the result is normal, ish is followed by the probe’s specific chromosomal region, such as 22q11.2, and then the name of the probe and the number of signals visualized—for example, HIRAx2.

51
00:06:57,080 --> 00:07:08,130
If a deletion is identified, del is included before the chromosomal region, and the name of the probe is followed by a minus sign (HIRA–), as shown in Table 16-1.

52
00:07:08,130 --> 00:07:14,330
The 22q11.2 deletion syndrome is discussed later (p. 315).

53
00:07:14,330 --> 00:07:23,355
For chromosomal microarray analysis (CMA), the designation begins with the abbreviation arr (p. 348).

54
00:07:23,355 --> 00:07:34,268
This is followed by the version of the genome build to which the nucleotide designations are aligned, such as GRCh38 for Genome Reference Consortium human build 38.

55
00:07:34,268 --> 00:07:43,580
Next, the number of the chromosome containing the abnormality is listed, followed by the p or q arm, and then the specific bands in question.

56
00:07:43,580 --> 00:07:52,993
CMA reports also include the affected base pair coordinates and thus convey the exact size and location for every abnormality identified .

57
00:07:52,993 --> 00:08:00,568
The information is reported in the same way whether the alteration is pathogenic or is of uncertain clinical significance.

58
00:08:00,568 --> 00:08:03,955
# Abnormalities of Chromosome Number.

59
00:08:03,955 --> 00:08:08,905
The most easily recognized chromosomal abnormalities are numerical.

60
00:08:08,905 --> 00:08:16,918
Aneuploidy is the inheritance of either an extra chromosome—resulting in trisomy, or loss of a chromosome—monosomy.

61
00:08:16,918 --> 00:08:23,093
This differs from polyploidy, which is an abnormal number of whole haploid chromosome sets.

62
00:08:23,093 --> 00:08:27,380
For example, triploidy has 69 chromosomes.

63
00:08:27,380 --> 00:08:34,630
The estimated incidence of various numerical chromosomal abnormalities is shown in Figure 16-1 .

64
00:08:34,630 --> 00:08:44,118
Autosomal Trisomies Trisomy usually results from nondisjunction, which is the failure of normal chromosomal pairing and separation during meiosis.

65
00:08:44,118 --> 00:08:54,555
Nondisjunction may occur if the chromosomes: (1) fail to pair up, (2) pair up properly but separate prematurely, or (3) fail to separate.

66
00:08:54,555 --> 00:09:05,080
Although each chromosome pair is equally likely to have a segregation error, trisomies other than 21, 18, or 13 rarely result in term pregnancies.

67
00:09:05,080 --> 00:09:10,105
Each of these autosomal trisomies has a spectrum of phenotypic severity.

68
00:09:10,105 --> 00:09:19,455
Some fetuses come to attention earlier in a pregnancy due to multiple major organ abnormalities or hydrops fetalis (p. 328).

69
00:09:19,455 --> 00:09:24,980
Others have more subtle findings or are detected only through prenatal screening tests .

70
00:09:24,980 --> 00:09:31,155
Survival data are based on cases with better prognosis, and this factors into counseling.

71
00:09:31,155 --> 00:09:37,355
The likelihood that a pregnancy will be complicated by an autosomal trisomy rises steeply with

72
00:09:37,516 --> 00:09:41,578
maternal age and particularly after age 35 .

73
00:09:41,528 --> 00:09:47,416
From birth until ovulation, oocytes are suspended in midprophase of meiosis I.

74
00:09:47,416 --> 00:09:57,291
If nondisjunction occurs after completion of meiosis, one gamete will have two copies of the affected chromosome, which leads to trisomy if fertilized.

75
00:09:57,291 --> 00:10:04,903
The other gamete, receiving no copy of the affected chromosome, is nullisomic and will be monosomic if fertilized.

76
00:10:04,903 --> 00:10:13,378
It is estimated that 10 to 20 percent of oocytes are aneuploid secondary to meiotic errors compared with 3 to 4 percent of sperm.

77
00:10:13,378 --> 00:10:24,591
After a pregnancy with an autosomal trisomy, the risk for any autosomal trisomy in a future pregnancy approximates 1 percent until the woman’s age-related risk exceeds this.

78
00:10:24,591 --> 00:10:32,503
Screening and prenatal diagnosis of autosomal trisomies are discussed in Chapter 17 (p. 333).

79
00:10:32,503 --> 00:10:43,203
Parental chromosomal studies are not indicated unless the abnormality was caused by an unbalanced translocation or other structural rearrangement (p. 315).

80
00:10:43,203 --> 00:10:45,653
Figure 16-2.

81
00:10:45,653 --> 00:10:56,728
Prevalence of autosomal trisomies according to maternal age found in population-based birth defect surveillance programs in the United States, 2006–2010.

82
00:10:56,728 --> 00:10:59,866
Trisomy 21—Down Syndrome.

83
00:10:59,866 --> 00:11:08,603
In 1866, J. L. H. Down described a group of intellectually disabled children with distinctive physical features.

84
00:11:08,603 --> 00:11:17,141
Nearly 100 years later, Lejeune (1959) demonstrated that Down syndrome stems from an autosomal trisomy .

85
00:11:17,141 --> 00:11:22,241
Trisomy 21 causes 95 percent of Down syndrome cases.

86
00:11:22,241 --> 00:11:30,666
The nondisjunction event occurs during meiosis I in approximately 75 percent of cases and in meiosis II in the remainder.

87
00:11:30,666 --> 00:11:41,866
Because chromosome 21 is acrocentric, a robertsonian translocation (p. 315) may occur, and this accounts for 3 to 4 percent of Down syndrome births.

88
00:11:41,866 --> 00:11:50,803
The remaining 1 to 2 percent are caused by isochromosome 21 or mosaicism for chromosome 21 (p. 317).

89
00:11:50,803 --> 00:11:53,266
Figure 16-3.

90
00:11:53,266 --> 00:12:01,253
Male karyotype with trisomy 21 (47,XY,+21), consistent with Down syndrome.

91
00:12:01,253 --> 00:12:05,216
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Prasad Koduru.

92
00:12:05,216 --> 00:12:09,103
Down syndrome is the most common nonlethal trisomy.

93
00:12:09,103 --> 00:12:14,041
It is identified in approximately 1 in 450 pregnancies .

94
00:12:14,041 --> 00:12:26,766
In the United States, fetal losses and pregnancy terminations yield an estimated prevalence of 1 case per 740 live births (Mai, 2013; Parker, 2010).

95
00:12:26,766 --> 00:12:31,941
The fetal death rate beyond 20 weeks’ gestation approximates 5 percent.

96
00:12:31,941 --> 00:12:51,016
Coinciding with the rise in overall number of pregnancies in women aged 35 years and older, the proportion of pregnancies with Down syndrome has increased approximately 33 percent during the past four decades (Loane, 2013; Parker, 2010; Shin, 2009).

97
00:12:51,016 --> 00:12:56,503
Most Down syndrome births at Parkland Hospital now occur in women in this age group .

98
00:12:56,503 --> 00:13:14,453
If major anomalies and minor aneuploidy markers are considered, 50 to 75 percent of pregnancies affected by Down syndrome are found to have a sonographic abnormality (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2020d; Hussamy, 2019).

99
00:13:14,453 --> 00:13:21,266
A major structural malformation is detected in the second trimester in approximately one third of cases.

100
00:13:21,266 --> 00:13:26,753
The most prevalent anomalies in affected children are cardiac and gastrointestinal.

101
00:13:26,753 --> 00:13:47,703
Cardiac abnormalities occur in approximately 50 percent, particularly endocardial cushion defects and ventricular septal defects (Figs. 15-39A and 15-39B, p. 291) (Bergstrom, 2016; Freeman, 2008; Stoll, 2015).

102
00:13:47,703 --> 00:14:04,616
Gastrointestinal abnormalities are identified in 6 to 12 percent and include esophageal atresia, duodenal atresia, and Hirschsprung disease (Fig. 15-48, p. 296) (Bull, 2011; Stoll, 2015).

103
00:14:04,616 --> 00:14:08,428
Other health problems are also common with Down syndrome.

104
00:14:08,428 --> 00:14:35,053
These include hearing loss in 75 percent, optical refractive errors in 50 percent, cataracts in 15 percent, obstructive sleep apnea in 60 percent, thyroid disease in 15 percent, transient myeloproliferative disorder in 10 percent of newborns, early-onset Alzheimer’s disease in 70 percent of adults, and a higher incidence of leukemia (Bull, 2011; Hartley, 2015).

105
00:14:35,053 --> 00:14:41,128
Women with Down syndrome are fertile, and nearly one third of their offspring will have Down syndrome.

106
00:14:41,128 --> 00:14:47,353
Males with Down syndrome have markedly reduced spermatogenesis and are almost always sterile.

107
00:14:47,353 --> 00:14:51,566
The average intelligence quotient (IQ) score is

108
00:14:51,724 --> 00:14:54,036
35 to 70.

109
00:14:53,986 --> 00:14:59,799
Social skills in affected children are usually higher than predicted by their IQ scores.

110
00:14:59,799 --> 00:15:05,086
Characteristic features of Down syndrome are shown in Figure 16-4.

111
00:15:05,086 --> 00:15:18,124
Typical findings include brachycephaly; epicanthal folds and up-slanting palpebral fissures; Brushfield spots, which are whitish spots on the periphery of the iris; a flat nasal bridge; and hypotonia.

112
00:15:18,124 --> 00:15:33,986
Infants often have loose skin at the nape of the neck, a prominent space or “sandal-gap” between the first and second toes, short fingers, a single palmar crease, and clinodactyly of the fifth digit, in which hypoplasia of the middle phalanx causes inward curvature.

113
00:15:33,986 --> 00:15:40,736
Discussed in Chapter 17, some of these findings are seen during prenatal ultrasound examination.

114
00:15:40,736 --> 00:15:43,236
Figure 16-4.

115
00:15:43,236 --> 00:15:50,399
Newborn with Down syndrome, karyotype 47,XX,+21 (trisomy 21).

116
00:15:50,399 --> 00:15:56,311
A. Characteristic facial features include epicanthal folds and a flattened nasal bridge.

117
00:15:56,311 --> 00:16:04,361
B. The hand has a single palmar crease (arrow) and hypoplasia of the middle phalanx of the fifth digit (arrowhead).

118
00:16:04,361 --> 00:16:10,086
C. The “sandal gap” between the first and second toes is also known as hallux varus.

119
00:16:10,086 --> 00:16:14,711
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Aldeboran N. Rodriguez.

120
00:16:14,711 --> 00:16:21,736
Data suggest that approximately 95 percent of liveborn infants with Down syndrome survive the first year.

121
00:16:21,736 --> 00:16:33,186
The 10-year survival rate is at least 90 percent overall and is 99 percent if major malformations are absent (Rankin, 2012; Vendola, 2010).

122
00:16:33,186 --> 00:16:37,411
Average life expectancy is 55 to 60 years.

123
00:16:37,411 --> 00:16:45,399
Several organizations offer education and support for prospective parents faced with prenatal diagnosis of Down syndrome.

124
00:16:45,399 --> 00:16:59,186
These include the March of Dimes, National Down Syndrome Congress (www.ndsccenter.org), and National Down Syndrome Society (www.ndss.org).

125
00:16:59,186 --> 00:17:02,336
Trisomy 18—Edwards Syndrome.

126
00:17:02,336 --> 00:17:11,149
The association between this constellation of abnormalities and an autosomal trisomy was first described by Edwards (1960).

127
00:17:11,149 --> 00:17:26,474
In population-based series of abortuses, stillbirths, and live births, trisomy 18 approximates 1 per 2000 recognized pregnancies (Goel, 2019; Loane, 2013; Parker, 2010).

128
00:17:26,474 --> 00:17:36,386
High in-utero lethality and termination of many affected pregnancies explains a live birth prevalence of just 1 per 6600 to 10,000.

129
00:17:36,386 --> 00:17:48,886
Unlike Down syndrome and Patau syndrome, which may result from a robertsonian translocation because they involve acrocentric chromosomes, Edwards syndrome uncommonly stems from a chromosomal rearrangement.

130
00:17:48,886 --> 00:17:53,211
Virtually every organ can be affected by trisomy 18.

131
00:17:53,211 --> 00:18:00,774
Common major anomalies include heart defects in more than 90 percent, particularly ventricular septal defects.

132
00:18:00,774 --> 00:18:17,849
Cerebellar vermian agenesis, myelomeningocele, diaphragmatic hernia, omphalocele, imperforate anus, and renal anomalies such as horseshoe kidney are others (Rosa, 2011; Springett, 2015; Watson, 2008).

133
00:18:17,849 --> 00:18:22,874
Sonographic images of these abnormalities are shown in Chapter 15.

134
00:18:22,874 --> 00:18:41,824
Frequent cranial and extremity abnormalities in affected fetuses include a “strawberry-shaped” cranium, abnormally wide cavum septum pellucidum, choroid plexus cysts, micrognathia, clenched hands with overlapping digits, radial aplasia with hyperflexed wrists, and rockerbottom or clubbed feet .

135
00:18:41,824 --> 00:18:54,449
Importantly, choroid plexus cysts only raise the risk for trisomy 18 in the setting of other risk factors, such as fetal structural abnormalities or an abnormal aneuploidy screening test result .

136
00:18:54,449 --> 00:18:57,024
Figure 16-5.

137
00:18:57,024 --> 00:19:00,174
Trisomy 18—Edwards Syndrome.

138
00:19:00,174 --> 00:19:08,236
A. This transventricular sonographic view shows fetal choroid plexus cysts and an angulated “strawberry-shaped” skull.

139
00:19:08,236 --> 00:19:18,311
B. Radial clubhand is manifested as a single forearm bone (radius), with the hands in a fixed, hyperflexed position at right angles to the forearms.

140
00:19:18,311 --> 00:19:27,261
C. This three-dimensional (3-D) sonographic image shows the characteristic hand position of clenched fist with overlapping digits.

141
00:19:27,261 --> 00:19:32,986
D. 3-D sonographic image displays a rockerbottom foot (vertical talus).

142
00:19:32,986 --> 00:19:46,736
Pregnancies with trisomy 18 that reach the third trimester often develop fetal-growth restriction, and the mean birthweight is <2500 g (Lin, 2006; Rosa, 2011).

143
00:19:46,736 --> 00:19:54,386
When undiagnosed, trisomy 18 has resulted in emergency cesarean for “fetal distress” in nearly 50

144
00:19:54,556 --> 00:20:00,518
percent of cases (Schneider, 1981; Houlihan, 2013).

145
00:20:00,468 --> 00:20:06,043
Mode of delivery and management of heart rate abnormalities should be discussed in advance.

146
00:20:06,043 --> 00:20:13,581
Discussed in Chapter 35 (p. 628), perinatal palliative care consultation should be offered .

147
00:20:13,581 --> 00:20:23,806
In the National Birth Defects Prevention Study, median neonatal survival with trisomy 18 was 8 days, and the 5-year survival rate was 12 percent .

148
00:20:23,806 --> 00:20:28,418
Not unexpectedly, major anomalies lower survival rates.

149
00:20:28,418 --> 00:20:40,393
Data from the Society of Thoracic Surgeons indicate that infants with trisomy 18 who undergo cardiac surgery have mortality rates 3 to 5 times higher than those without trisomy 18 .

150
00:20:40,393 --> 00:20:47,668
Thus, among the minority with trisomy 18 who survive until birth, the condition is severely life limiting.

151
00:20:47,668 --> 00:20:50,806
Trisomy 13—Patau Syndrome.

152
00:20:50,806 --> 00:21:00,281
This constellation of fetal abnormalities and their association with an autosomal trisomy was first described by Patau and colleagues (1960).

153
00:21:00,281 --> 00:21:09,218
The incidence of trisomy 13 approximates 1 case in 5000 recognized pregnancies, which includes abortuses and stillbirths.

154
00:21:09,218 --> 00:21:12,893
Most affected fetuses are lost or terminated.

155
00:21:12,893 --> 00:21:23,268
The live birth prevalence is 1 per 12,000 to 18,000 (Goel, 2019; Loane, 2013; Parker, 2010).

156
00:21:23,268 --> 00:21:28,956
At least 80 percent of pregnancies with Patau syndrome result from trisomy 13.

157
00:21:28,956 --> 00:21:36,218
With rare exception, the remainder are caused by a robertsonian translocation involving chromosome 13.

158
00:21:36,218 --> 00:21:50,568
The most common translocation involves chromosomes 13 and 14, der(13;14)(q10;q10), which is carried by approximately 1 in 1300 phenotypically normal individuals.

159
00:21:50,568 --> 00:21:57,343
Among translocation carriers, fewer than 2 percent give birth to a live infant with Patau syndrome.

160
00:21:57,343 --> 00:22:02,856
Trisomy 13 may be associated with abnormalities of any organ system.

161
00:22:02,856 --> 00:22:08,656
Abnormalities of the brain, heart, kidneys, and extremities are the most frequent.

162
00:22:08,656 --> 00:22:21,243
Holoprosencephaly, usually the alobar type, is present in two thirds of cases and often is accompanied by severe facial abnormalities (Fig. 1513, p. 279).

163
00:22:21,243 --> 00:22:30,406
These may include hypotelorism or cyclopia, microphthalmia, and nasal abnormalities that range from a single nostril to a proboscis.

164
00:22:30,406 --> 00:22:34,156
Cardiac defects are found in up to 90 percent .

165
00:22:34,156 --> 00:22:52,843
Other abnormalities that suggest trisomy 13 include cephalocele, microcephaly, omphalocele, cystic renal dysplasia, polydactyly, rockerbottom feet, aplasia cutis, and cleft lip-palate, which may be median (Lin, 2007; Springett, 2015).

166
00:22:52,843 --> 00:22:57,481
Sonographic images of several of these are shown in Chapter 15.

167
00:22:57,481 --> 00:23:03,293
Affected fetuses often also have bilateral echogenic intracardiac foci.

168
00:23:03,293 --> 00:23:15,043
For the fetus or newborn with a cephalocele, cystic kidneys, and polydactyly, the differential diagnosis includes trisomy 13 and the autosomal-recessive Meckel–Gruber syndrome.

169
00:23:15,043 --> 00:23:20,231
Trisomy 13 is life limiting, even more so than trisomy 18 .

170
00:23:20,231 --> 00:23:30,768
In the National Birth Defects Prevention Study, median survival of neonates with trisomy 13 was 5 days, and the 5-year survival rate was below 10 percent .

171
00:23:30,768 --> 00:23:37,043
Occasionally children with trisomy 13 have been candidates for palliative surgical procedures.

172
00:23:37,043 --> 00:23:45,531
Most of these have a milder phenotype that lacks brain, cardiac, gastrointestinal, or genitourinary abnormalities .

173
00:23:45,531 --> 00:23:52,756
Counseling regarding prenatal diagnosis and management options is similar to that described for trisomy 18.

174
00:23:52,756 --> 00:23:58,743
Unlike other aneuploidies, fetal trisomy 13 confers risk to the pregnant woman.

175
00:23:58,743 --> 00:24:08,943
Continuing pregnancies have at least a 25-percent risk for hypertensive complications (Dotters-Katz, 2018; Tuohy, 1992).

176
00:24:08,943 --> 00:24:17,218
The risk for preeclampsia with severe features is increased more than tenfold and often develops prior to 32 weeks’ gestation.

177
00:24:17,218 --> 00:24:28,868
Chromosome 13 contains the gene for soluble fms-like tyrosine kinase-1 (sFlt-1), which is an antiangiogenic protein associated with preeclampsia .

178
00:24:28,868 --> 00:24:42,443
Investigators have documented overexpression of the sFlt-1 protein by trisomic 13 placentas and in serum of women with preeclampsia (Bdolah, 2006; Silasi, 2011).

179
00:24:42,443 --> 00:24:44,443
Other Trisomies.

180
00:24:44,443 --> 00:24:49,231
Live births are extremely rare with any other autosomal trisomy.

181
00:24:49,231 --> 00:24:57,806
Trisomy 22 has been described in case reports and series (Heinrich, 2013; Kehinde, 2014).

182
00:24:57,806 --> 00:25:05,156
Affected fetuses have severe growth restriction, microcephaly with an abnormal-shaped cranium, midface

183
00:25:05,308 --> 00:25:10,133
hypoplasia, and cardiac and genitourinary abnormalities.

184
00:25:10,083 --> 00:25:17,383
Trisomy 16 is the most prevalent trisomy among first-trimester losses, accounting for 16 percent.

185
00:25:17,383 --> 00:25:23,045
Trisomies 21 and 22 are also common among first trimester losses.

186
00:25:23,045 --> 00:25:26,045
Trisomy 1 has never been reported.

187
00:25:26,045 --> 00:25:32,070
Monosomy Nondisjunction creates an equal number of nullisomic and disomic gametes.

188
00:25:32,070 --> 00:25:42,645
As a rule, missing chromosomal material is more devastating than extra chromosomal material, and almost all monosomic conceptuses are lost before implantation.

189
00:25:42,645 --> 00:25:50,533
The one exception is monosomy for the X chromosome (45,X), Turner syndrome, which is discussed subsequently.

190
00:25:50,533 --> 00:25:59,095
Unlike autosomal trisomies, which increase in incidence with maternal age, monosomy and maternal age lack an association.

191
00:25:59,095 --> 00:26:04,383
Polyploidy This is defined as more than two complete haploid chromosomal sets.

192
00:26:04,383 --> 00:26:11,833
Polyploidy accounts for approximately 20 percent of spontaneous abortions but is rarely found later in gestation.

193
00:26:11,833 --> 00:26:17,045
Triploid pregnancies have three haploid sets, 69 chromosomes.

194
00:26:17,045 --> 00:26:27,920
If extra set of chromosomes is paternal, the result is diandric triploidy, and if maternal, digynic triploidy, relevant because the phenotype reflects the parent of origin.

195
00:26:27,920 --> 00:26:32,020
This is an example of imprinting (p. 322).

196
00:26:32,020 --> 00:26:40,008
Diandric or type I triploidy occurs after fertilization of one egg by two sperm or by one abnormal diploid sperm.

197
00:26:40,008 --> 00:26:47,370
Diandric triploidy produces a partial molar pregnancy, discussed in Chapter 13 (p. 237).

198
00:26:47,370 --> 00:26:57,695
With digynic or type II triploidy, the extra chromosomal set is maternal, and the egg fails to undergo the first or second meiotic division before fertilization.

199
00:26:57,695 --> 00:27:02,020
Digynic triploid placentas do not develop molar changes.

200
00:27:02,020 --> 00:27:06,945
However, the fetus usually develops asymmetrical growth restriction.

201
00:27:06,945 --> 00:27:21,208
Although diandric triploidy accounts for the majority of triploid conceptions, the early loss rate is so high that two thirds of triploid pregnancies identified beyond the first trimester are digynic ( Jauniaux, 1999).

202
00:27:21,208 --> 00:27:25,645
Triploidy is recognized in 1 per 5000 pregnancies .

203
00:27:25,645 --> 00:27:33,533
It is considered lethal, and fetuses with either the diandric or digynic form typically have multiple structural anomalies.

204
00:27:33,533 --> 00:27:42,620
The brain, heart, kidneys, and extremities are commonly affected (Massalska, 2017; Zalel, 2016).

205
00:27:42,620 --> 00:27:50,120
Counseling, prenatal diagnosis, and delivery management are similar to those for trisomies 18 and 13.

206
00:27:50,120 --> 00:28:01,483
The recurrence risk for a woman whose triploid fetus survived past the first trimester is 1 to 1.5 percent, and thus prenatal diagnosis is offered in future pregnancies.

207
00:28:01,483 --> 00:28:10,608
Tetraploid pregnancies have four haploid sets of chromosomes, resulting in either 92,XXXX or 92,XXYY.

208
00:28:10,608 --> 00:28:15,795
This suggests a postzygotic failure to complete an early cleavage division.

209
00:28:15,795 --> 00:28:20,695
The conceptus invariably succumbs, and the recurrence risk is minimal.

210
00:28:20,695 --> 00:28:25,520
Sex Chromosome Abnormalities 45,X—Turner Syndrome.

211
00:28:25,520 --> 00:28:32,795
First described by Turner (1938), this syndrome later was found to be caused by monosomy X .

212
00:28:32,795 --> 00:28:41,870
The birth prevalence of Turner syndrome is approximately 1 in 2500 girls (Cragan, 2009; Dolk, 2010).

213
00:28:41,870 --> 00:28:50,783
The missing X chromosome is paternally derived in 80 percent of cases (Cockwell, 1991; Hassold, 1990).

214
00:28:50,783 --> 00:29:00,758
Turner syndrome is the only monosomy compatible with life, but it is also the most common aneuploidy in first-trimester losses, accounting for 20 percent.

215
00:29:00,758 --> 00:29:04,520
This is explained by its wide range in phenotype.

216
00:29:04,520 --> 00:29:11,920
Approximately 98 percent of affected conceptuses are so abnormal that they abort early in the first trimester.

217
00:29:11,920 --> 00:29:23,308
Of the remainder, many manifest large, septated cystic hygromas in the late first or early second trimester, often in the setting of edema that progresses to hydrops fetalis.

218
00:29:23,308 --> 00:29:29,170
When cystic hygromas are accompanied by hydrops, the prognosis is extremely poor .

219
00:29:29,170 --> 00:29:34,645
Fewer than 1 percent of pregnancies with Turner syndrome result in a liveborn neonate.

220
00:29:34,645 --> 00:29:51,420
Of these, only half are actually monosomy X— approximately a fourth have mosaicism for monosomy X, such as 45,X/46,XX or 45,X/46,XY, and another 15 percent have isochromosome X.

221
00:29:51,420 --> 00:30:03,283
It is thought that surviving individuals with 45,X may have had the benefit of “rescue” by an additional cell line containing 46,XX during critical phases of development that was subsequently

222
00:30:03,436 --> 00:30:04,973
lost .

223
00:30:04,923 --> 00:30:23,411
Abnormalities associated with Turner syndrome include left-sided cardiac defects—such as coarctation of the aorta, hypoplastic left heart syndrome, or bicuspid aortic valve—in 30 to 50 percent; renal anomalies, particularly horseshoe kidney; and hypothyroidism.

224
00:30:23,411 --> 00:30:36,298
Other features are short stature, broad chest with widely spaced nipples, congenital lymphedema— puffiness over the dorsum of hands and feet, and a “webbed” posterior neck resulting from cystic hygromas.

225
00:30:36,298 --> 00:30:47,248
Intelligence scores are generally in the normal range, but there may be impairments in visual-spatial organization, nonverbal problem solving, and interpretation of social cues.

226
00:30:47,248 --> 00:30:54,961
A consensus guideline is available that addresses screening and treatment for the range of health problems affected individuals face .

227
00:30:54,961 --> 00:31:00,173
Growth hormone is typically administered in childhood to ameliorate short stature.

228
00:31:00,173 --> 00:31:06,436
More than 90 percent have ovarian dysgenesis and require estrogen repletion at puberty.

229
00:31:06,436 --> 00:31:17,411
An exception is mosaicism involving the Y chromosome, as this confers risk for germ cell neoplasm—regardless of whether the child is phenotypically male or female.

230
00:31:17,411 --> 00:31:26,186
Accordingly, eventual prophylactic bilateral gonadectomy is indicated (Cools, 2011; Schorge, 2020).

231
00:31:26,186 --> 00:31:28,748
47,XXX.

232
00:31:28,748 --> 00:31:36,036
Approximately 1 in 1000 female newborns has an additional X chromosome—47,XXX .

233
00:31:36,036 --> 00:31:41,036
The extra X is maternally derived in more than 90 percent of cases.

234
00:31:41,036 --> 00:31:50,536
The prevalence of 47,XXX is weakly associated with maternal age, and cell-free DNA screening has resulted in increased diagnoses.

235
00:31:50,536 --> 00:31:58,661
No specific pattern of malformations has been described, but genitourinary problems and seizure disorders are more common .

236
00:31:58,661 --> 00:32:02,636
Affected infants do not have a characteristic appearance.

237
00:32:02,636 --> 00:32:17,336
When children do come to attention, features may include tall stature, hypertelorism, epicanthal folds, kyphoscoliosis, clinodactyly, and hypotonia (Tartaglia, 2010; Wigby, 2016).

238
00:32:17,336 --> 00:32:26,986
More than one third are diagnosed with a learning disability, half have attention deficit disorder, and overall cognitive scores are in the low-average range.

239
00:32:26,986 --> 00:32:29,811
Pubertal development is unaffected.

240
00:32:29,811 --> 00:32:33,648
Primary ovarian insufficiency has been reported.

241
00:32:33,648 --> 00:32:42,511
In the absence of prenatal diagnosis, it is estimated that 47,XXX is ascertained in only 10 percent of affected children .

242
00:32:42,511 --> 00:32:53,773
Females with two or more extra X chromosomes—48,XXXX or 49,XXXXX—are likely to have physical abnormalities apparent at birth.

243
00:32:53,773 --> 00:32:59,023
These abnormal X complements are associated with intellectual disability.

244
00:32:59,023 --> 00:33:05,123
For both males and females, the IQ score is lower with each additional X chromosome.

245
00:33:05,123 --> 00:33:09,061
47,XXY—Klinefelter Syndrome.

246
00:33:09,061 --> 00:33:12,898
This is the most common sex chromosome abnormality.

247
00:33:12,898 --> 00:33:17,286
It occurs in approximately 1 in 700 male infants .

248
00:33:17,286 --> 00:33:26,823
The additional X chromosome is maternally or paternally derived with equal propensity (Jacobs, 1995; Lowe, 2001).

249
00:33:26,823 --> 00:33:31,423
There is a weak association with advanced maternal and paternal age.

250
00:33:31,423 --> 00:33:41,223
Like 47,XXX, newborns with 47,XXY usually appear phenotypically normal and do not have a higher incidence of anomalies.

251
00:33:41,223 --> 00:33:47,123
As children, boys are typically taller than average and have normal prepubertal development.

252
00:33:47,123 --> 00:33:55,298
However, they have gonadal dysgenesis, do not undergo normal virilization, and require testosterone supplementation.

253
00:33:55,298 --> 00:33:57,998
They may develop gynecomastia.

254
00:33:57,998 --> 00:34:09,111
IQ scores usually lie in the average to low-average range, with increased rates of delayed language development (Boada, 2009; Girardin, 2011).

255
00:34:09,111 --> 00:34:14,923
Initiation of hormone replacement was previously recommended to begin in adolescence.

256
00:34:14,923 --> 00:34:29,861
However, more recent research suggests that therapy earlier in childhood results in improved working memory and executive functioning and a decrease in anxiety disorders (Samango-Sprouse 2019; Tran, 2019).

257
00:34:29,861 --> 00:34:32,523
47,XYY.

258
00:34:32,523 --> 00:34:37,748
This aneuploidy occurs in approximately 1 in 1000 male newborns .

259
00:34:37,748 --> 00:34:44,123
As with 47,XXX and XXY individuals, affected boys tend to be tall.

260
00:34:44,123 --> 00:34:50,536
A third have macrocephaly, nearly two thirds demonstrate hypotonia, and tremors are common .

261
00:34:50,536 --> 00:34:58,486
Rates of major anomalies are not elevated, although hypertelorism and clinodactyly may be identified in more than half.

262
00:34:58,486 --> 00:35:02,836
Pubertal development is normal, and fertility is unimpaired.

263
00:35:02,836 --> 00:35:05,373
Affected individuals do

264
00:35:05,524 --> 00:35:18,499
have increased rates of oral and written language impairments, attention deficit disorder, developmental delays, and autism spectrum disorder (Bardsley, 2013; Joseph, 2018).

265
00:35:18,449 --> 00:35:36,724
Males with more than two Y chromosomes—48,XYYY—or with both additional X and Y chromosomes—48,XXYY or 49,XXXYY—are more likely to have congenital abnormalities, medical problems, and intellectual disability .

266
00:35:36,724 --> 00:35:40,324
# Abnormalities of Chromosome Structure.

267
00:35:40,324 --> 00:35:51,599
Structural chromosomal abnormalities include deletions, duplications, translocations, isochromosomes, inversions, ring chromosomes, and mosaicism .

268
00:35:51,599 --> 00:35:58,449
Identification of a structural chromosomal abnormality in offspring raises two primary questions.

269
00:35:58,449 --> 00:36:04,736
First, what phenotypic or later developmental abnormalities are associated with the finding?

270
00:36:04,736 --> 00:36:09,136
Second, is parental karyotype evaluation indicated?

271
00:36:09,136 --> 00:36:18,049
In other words, are the parents at increased risk of carrying this abnormality, and if so, what is their risk of having future affected offspring?

272
00:36:18,049 --> 00:36:27,786
Deletions and Duplications A chromosome with a deletion has a stretch of DNA that is missing, whereas one with a duplication has a region that is included twice.

273
00:36:27,786 --> 00:36:36,499
Most deletions and duplications occur during meiosis and result from malalignment or mismatching during the pairing of homologous chromosomes.

274
00:36:36,499 --> 00:36:47,461
The misaligned segment may then be deleted, or if the mismatch remains when the two chromosomes recombine, it may result in a deletion in one chromosome and duplication in the other .

275
00:36:47,461 --> 00:37:01,836
When a deletion or duplication is identified in a fetus or infant, parental karyotyping should be offered, because if either parent carries a balanced translocation, the recurrence risk in subsequent pregnancies is significantly increased.

276
00:37:01,836 --> 00:37:11,799
Deletions involving DNA segments large enough to be seen with standard cytogenetic karyotyping are identified in approximately 1 in 7000 births.

277
00:37:11,799 --> 00:37:19,524
Common deletions may be referred to by eponyms—for example, del 5p is called cri du chat syndrome.

278
00:37:19,524 --> 00:37:22,036
Figure 16-6.

279
00:37:22,036 --> 00:37:29,499
A mismatch during pairing of homologous chromosomes may lead to a deletion in one chromosome and a duplication in the other.

280
00:37:29,499 --> 00:37:33,424
Del = deletion; Dupl = duplication.

281
00:37:33,424 --> 00:37:36,799
Microdeletions and Microduplications.

282
00:37:36,799 --> 00:37:46,024
When a deletion or duplication is smaller than 3 to 5 million base pairs, it is too small to be detected with a standard karyotype analysis.

283
00:37:46,024 --> 00:37:53,249
CMA permits identification of these microdeletions and microduplications (p. 325).

284
00:37:53,249 --> 00:38:00,711
When CMA is used, the region of DNA that is missing or duplicated is termed a genomic copy number variant.

285
00:38:00,711 --> 00:38:06,811
A microdeletion or duplication may involve a stretch of DNA that contains multiple genes.

286
00:38:06,811 --> 00:38:14,061
This causes a contiguous gene syndrome, which can encompass serious but unrelated phenotypic abnormalities .

287
00:38:14,061 --> 00:38:22,111
In some cases, a microduplication may involve the exact DNA region that causes a recognized microdeletion syndrome .

288
00:38:22,111 --> 00:38:29,411
When a specific microdeletion syndrome is suspected clinically, it is confirmed using either CMA or FISH.

289
00:38:29,411 --> 00:38:31,961
Table 16-2.

290
00:38:31,961 --> 00:38:38,161
Selected Microdeletion Syndromes 22q11.2 Microdeletion Syndrome.

291
00:38:38,161 --> 00:38:44,961
This syndrome is also known as DiGeorge syndrome, Shprintzen syndrome, and velocardiofacial syndrome.

292
00:38:44,961 --> 00:38:51,674
It is the most common microdeletion, with a prevalence of 1 case in 3000 to 6000 births.

293
00:38:51,674 --> 00:38:59,461
Inheritance is autosomal dominant—offspring of affected individuals have a 50-percent chance of inheriting the syndrome.

294
00:38:59,461 --> 00:39:04,724
However, more than 90 percent of cases arise from a de-novo mutation.

295
00:39:04,724 --> 00:39:13,111
The full deletion contains 3 million base pairs, encompasses 40 genes, and includes 180 different features .

296
00:39:13,111 --> 00:39:21,936
This is emblematic of a contiguous gene syndrome and poses counseling challenges because features can vary widely, even among family members.

297
00:39:21,936 --> 00:39:29,061
With prenatal diagnosis of an affected fetus, genetic testing is offered to the pregnant patient and her partner.

298
00:39:29,061 --> 00:39:43,361
Approximately 75 percent of affected individuals have an associated conotruncal cardiac anomaly, such as tetralogy of Fallot, truncus arteriosus, interrupted aortic arch, or ventricular septal defect .

299
00:39:43,361 --> 00:39:49,711
Immune deficiency, such as T-cell lymphopenia, also develops in 75 percent.

300
00:39:49,711 --> 00:39:55,011
More than 70 percent have velopharyngeal insufficiency or cleft palate.

301
00:39:55,180 --> 00:40:05,780
Characteristic facial features include short palpebral fissures, bulbous nasal tip, micrognathia, short philtrum, and small or posteriorly rotated ears.

302
00:40:05,730 --> 00:40:12,280
Learning disabilities, autism spectrum disorder, and intellectual disability also are common.

303
00:40:12,280 --> 00:40:24,355
Other manifestations include hypocalcemia, renal anomalies, esophageal dysmotility, hearing loss, behavioral disorders, and psychiatric illness, particularly schizophrenia.

304
00:40:24,355 --> 00:40:33,117
Chromosomal Translocations These are DNA rearrangements in which a DNA segment breaks away from one chromosome and attaches to another.

305
00:40:33,117 --> 00:40:38,142
The rearranged chromosomes are called derivative (der) chromosomes.

306
00:40:38,142 --> 00:40:42,580
The two types are reciprocal and robertsonian translocations.

307
00:40:42,580 --> 00:40:45,255
Reciprocal Translocations.

308
00:40:45,255 --> 00:40:53,530
A double-segment or reciprocal translocation results when breaks occur in two different chromosomes and the broken fragments are exchanged.

309
00:40:53,530 --> 00:40:57,367
Each affected chromosome receives a fragment of the other.

310
00:40:57,367 --> 00:41:03,530
If no chromosomal material is gained or lost, the translocation is considered balanced.

311
00:41:03,530 --> 00:41:09,030
The prevalence of reciprocal translocations approximates 1 in 600 births.

312
00:41:09,030 --> 00:41:14,155
The carrier of a balanced translocation will usually have a normal phenotype.

313
00:41:14,155 --> 00:41:27,117
However, repositioning of specific genes within chromosomal segments may cause major structural or developmental abnormalities in approximately 6 percent of apparently balanced translocation carriers.

314
00:41:27,117 --> 00:41:37,880
Using CMA technology, up to 20 percent of individuals who appear to have a balanced translocation are found instead to have missing or redundant DNA segments .

315
00:41:37,880 --> 00:41:45,255
Balanced translocation carriers are at risk to produce unbalanced gametes, resulting in abnormal offspring.

316
00:41:45,255 --> 00:42:00,167
As shown in Figure 16-7, if an oocyte or sperm contains a translocated chromosome, fertilization results in an unbalanced translocation—monosomy for part of one affected chromosome and trisomy for part of the other.

317
00:42:00,167 --> 00:42:12,005
In general, translocation carriers identified after the birth of an abnormal child have a 5- to 30-percent risk of producing liveborn offspring with an unbalanced translocation.

318
00:42:12,005 --> 00:42:20,342
Carriers identified for other reasons, for example, during an infertility evaluation, have only a 5-percent risk.

319
00:42:20,342 --> 00:42:25,980
This risk is lower because gametes are so abnormal that conceptions are nonviable.

320
00:42:25,980 --> 00:42:28,480
Figure 16-7.

321
00:42:28,480 --> 00:42:43,117
A carrier of a balanced translocation may produce offspring who are also carriers of the balanced rearrangement (B), offspring with unbalanced translocations (C, D), or offspring with normal chromosomal complements (A).

322
00:42:43,117 --> 00:42:45,942
Robertsonian Translocations.

323
00:42:45,942 --> 00:42:53,780
These involve only the acrocentric chromosomes—13, 14, 15, 21, and 22.

324
00:42:53,780 --> 00:43:02,680
Acrocentric chromosomes have extremely short p arms, and the p arms contain redundant copies of genes coding for ribosomal RNA.

325
00:43:02,680 --> 00:43:14,505
In a robertsonian translocation, the q arms of two acrocentric chromosomes fuse at one centromere to form a derivative chromosome, and the other centromere and both p arms are lost.

326
00:43:14,505 --> 00:43:24,155
The lost DNA is present in multiple copies on other acrocentric chromosomes, and thus the translocation carrier is usually phenotypically normal.

327
00:43:24,155 --> 00:43:32,955
Because the number of centromeres determines the chromosome count, a robertsonian translocation carrier has only 45 chromosomes.

328
00:43:32,955 --> 00:43:37,342
Balanced robertsonian carriers have reproductive difficulties.

329
00:43:37,342 --> 00:43:45,805
During fertilization, if the derivative chromosome pairs with a normal haploid chromosome, the resulting offspring will have trisomy.

330
00:43:45,805 --> 00:43:54,280
If the fused chromosomes are homologous, that is, from the same chromosome pair, the carrier can produce only unbalanced gametes.

331
00:43:54,280 --> 00:44:04,680
Each egg or sperm contains either both copies of the translocated chromosome, which would result in trisomy if fertilized, or no copy, which would result in monosomy.

332
00:44:04,680 --> 00:44:11,042
If the fused chromosomes are nonhomologous, four of the six possible gametes would be abnormal.

333
00:44:11,042 --> 00:44:16,230
Robertsonian translocations are found in 1 in 1000 individuals.

334
00:44:16,230 --> 00:44:25,967
The risk to have an abnormal offspring approximates 15 percent if a robertsonian translocation is carried by the mother and 2 percent if carried by the father.

335
00:44:25,967 --> 00:44:35,455
However, robertsonian translocations are not a major cause of miscarriage and are identified in fewer than 5 percent of couples evaluated for

336
00:44:35,620 --> 00:44:38,045
recurrent pregnancy loss.

337
00:44:37,995 --> 00:44:45,620
If a fetus or child is found to have a translocation trisomy, both parents should be offered karyotype analysis.

338
00:44:45,620 --> 00:44:50,545
If neither parent is a carrier, the recurrence risk is extremely low.

339
00:44:50,545 --> 00:45:03,057
The most common robertsonian translocation is der(13;14)(q10;q10), which accounts for up to 20 percent of cases of Patau syndrome (p. 313).

340
00:45:03,057 --> 00:45:11,157
Isochromosomes When either two p arms or two q arms from the same chromosome fuse together, an isochromosome results.

341
00:45:11,157 --> 00:45:18,195
This may occur if the centromere breaks transversely instead of longitudinally during meiosis II or mitosis.

342
00:45:18,195 --> 00:45:25,545
Alternately, an isochromosome can result from a meiotic error in a chromosome with a robertsonian translocation.

343
00:45:25,545 --> 00:45:34,107
If the isochromosome is acrocentric, it is composed of two q arms and will behave like a homologous robertsonian translocation.

344
00:45:34,107 --> 00:45:40,407
Such a carrier is phenotypically normal but can produce only abnormal, unbalanced gametes.

345
00:45:40,407 --> 00:45:47,370
If the isochromosome is nonacrocentric, it may be composed of either two p arms or two q arms.

346
00:45:47,370 --> 00:45:54,907
Functionally this results in trisomy of the genes on the arms that are present and monosomy of the genes on the arms that are lost.

347
00:45:54,907 --> 00:46:01,245
Thus, the carrier is usually phenotypically abnormal and produces abnormal gametes.

348
00:46:01,245 --> 00:46:11,232
The most common isochromosome involves the long arm of the X chromosome, i(Xq), which is the etiology of 15 percent of cases of Turner syndrome.

349
00:46:11,232 --> 00:46:20,032
Chromosomal Inversions When the same chromosome breaks in two places, the intervening genetic material may invert before the breaks are repaired.

350
00:46:20,032 --> 00:46:26,145
Although no genetic material is lost or duplicated, an inversion may alter gene function.

351
00:46:26,145 --> 00:46:30,595
There are two types—pericentric and paracentric inversions.

352
00:46:30,595 --> 00:46:32,895
Pericentric Inversion.

353
00:46:32,895 --> 00:46:40,557
This results from breaks in both the p and q arms of a chromosome, such that the inverted material spans the centromere .

354
00:46:40,557 --> 00:46:51,670
A pericentric inversion causes problems in chromosomal alignment during meiosis and confers significant risk for the carrier to produce abnormal gametes and abnormal offspring.

355
00:46:51,670 --> 00:46:59,520
The observed risk of abnormal offspring is 5 to 10 percent if ascertainment is made after the birth of an abnormal child.

356
00:46:59,520 --> 00:47:04,332
The risk is only 1 to 3 percent if prompted by another indication.

357
00:47:04,332 --> 00:47:16,520
An important exception is inv(9)(p11q12), a pericentric inversion on chromosome 9 that is a normal variant and present in approximately 1 percent of the population.

358
00:47:16,520 --> 00:47:18,932
Figure 16-8.

359
00:47:18,932 --> 00:47:24,907
Pericentric inversion involves the centromere, whereas paracentric inversion does not.

360
00:47:24,907 --> 00:47:31,607
Individuals with pericentric inversions are at risk to have offspring with a duplication/deletion.

361
00:47:31,607 --> 00:47:37,120
Those with paracentric inversions are at increased risk for early pregnancy loss.

362
00:47:37,120 --> 00:47:39,445
Paracentric Inversion.

363
00:47:39,445 --> 00:47:50,020
If one arm of a chromosome suffers the two breaks, the inverted material does not include the centromere, and the inversion is paracentric (see Fig. 16-8).

364
00:47:50,020 --> 00:47:56,920
The carrier makes either normal balanced gametes or gametes that are so abnormal as to preclude fertilization.

365
00:47:56,920 --> 00:48:03,920
Thus, although infertility may be a problem, the risk of having an abnormal offspring is extremely low.

366
00:48:03,920 --> 00:48:11,520
Ring Chromosome If a deletion occurs at each end of the same chromosome, the ends may come together to form a ring chromosome.

367
00:48:11,520 --> 00:48:19,232
The telomere regions at the ends of each chromosome contain redundant nucleoprotein complexes that stabilize the chromosome.

368
00:48:19,232 --> 00:48:26,245
If only the telomeres are lost, all necessary genetic material is retained, and the carrier is balanced.

369
00:48:26,245 --> 00:48:33,170
If a deletion extends more proximally than the telomere, the carrier is likely to be phenotypically abnormal.

370
00:48:33,170 --> 00:48:38,620
An example of this is the ring X chromosome, which may result in Turner syndrome.

371
00:48:38,620 --> 00:48:40,982
# Mosaicism.

372
00:48:40,982 --> 00:48:47,970
A mosaic individual has two or more cytogenetically distinct cell lines that are derived from a single zygote.

373
00:48:47,970 --> 00:49:00,695
Phenotypic expression of mosaicism depends on several factors, including whether the cytogenetically abnormal cells involve the fetus, part of the fetus, just the placenta, or some combination.

374
00:49:00,695 --> 00:49:06,995
Mosaicism is found in approximately 0.3 percent of amnionic fluid cultures .

375
00:49:06,995 --> 00:49:15,957
When abnormal cells are present in only a single flask of amnionic fluid, the finding is likely pseudomosaicism, caused by cell-culture

376
00:49:16,108 --> 00:49:21,033
artifact (Bui, 1984; Hsu, 1984).

377
00:49:20,983 --> 00:49:27,295
When abnormal cells involve multiple cultures, however, true mosaicism is more likely.

378
00:49:27,295 --> 00:49:37,045
Further testing verifies a second cell line in 60 to 70 percent of these fetuses (Hsu, 1984; Worton, 1984).

379
00:49:37,045 --> 00:49:46,620
Confined Placental Mosaicism Mosaicism involving a gene abnormality is detected in 1 to 2 percent of chorionic villus sampling specimens.

380
00:49:46,620 --> 00:49:49,445
Amniocentesis should be offered.

381
00:49:49,445 --> 00:50:00,845
In a series of more than 1000 pregnancies with mosaicism at chorionic villus sampling, subsequent amniocentesis identified true fetal mosaicism in 13 percent.

382
00:50:00,845 --> 00:50:11,645
Confined placental mosaicism accounted for 85 percent of cases, and uniparental disomy, discussed later (p. 322), was found in 2 percent .

383
00:50:11,645 --> 00:50:26,820
If mosaicism is detected for a chromosome known to contain imprinted genes—such as chromosomes 6, 7, 11, 14, or 15—testing for uniparental disomy should be considered, as there may be fetal consequences .

384
00:50:26,820 --> 00:50:34,895
Although outcomes with confined placental mosaicism are generally good, risks of fetal-growth restriction and stillbirth are higher .

385
00:50:34,895 --> 00:50:40,645
Fetal-growth restriction may stem from impaired functioning of the aneuploid placental cells .

386
00:50:40,645 --> 00:50:56,708
In a recent series of 5500 pregnancies undergoing chorionic villus sampling, the preterm birth rate in pregnancies with confined placental mosaicism was 45 percent, and 50 percent of affected newborns were small for gestational age .

387
00:50:56,708 --> 00:51:06,645
Placental mosaicism for trisomy 16 confers a particularly poor prognosis, with as few as 1 in 3 pregnancies resulting in normal outcome .

388
00:51:06,645 --> 00:51:15,608
Gonadal Mosaicism Also called germline mosaicism, this refers to having one cell line in gametes and another in somatic cells.

389
00:51:15,608 --> 00:51:21,658
A genetic abnormality that is confined to gamete cells will affect all cells in the offspring.

390
00:51:21,658 --> 00:51:28,708
Thus, gonadal mosaicism can account for apparently de-novo diseases in the offspring of normal parents.

391
00:51:28,708 --> 00:51:40,570
Because spermatogonia and oogonia divide throughout fetal life, and spermatogonia continue to divide throughout adulthood, a meiotic error can occur in germ cells that were previously normal.

392
00:51:40,570 --> 00:51:50,033
New mutations identified in an offspring whose father is older than 40 may arise through this mechanism, as discussed later (p. 319) .

393
00:51:50,033 --> 00:51:58,333
Gonadal mosaicism also explains the 6-percent recurrence risk after the birth of a child with a disease caused by a “new” mutation.

394
00:51:58,333 --> 00:52:00,533
Modes Of Inheritance.

395
00:52:00,533 --> 00:52:09,645
A monogenic or mendelian disorder is caused by a mutation or alteration in a single locus or gene in one or both members of a gene pair.

396
00:52:09,645 --> 00:52:16,933
Types of mendelian inheritance include autosomal dominant, autosomal recessive, Xlinked, and Y-linked .

397
00:52:16,933 --> 00:52:26,533
Other monogenic inheritance patterns include mitochondrial inheritance, uniparental disomy, imprinting, and trinucleotide repeat expansion.

398
00:52:26,533 --> 00:52:29,020
Table 16-3.

399
00:52:29,020 --> 00:52:36,008
Selected Monogenic (Mendelian) Disorders # Relationship between Phenotype and Genotype.

400
00:52:36,008 --> 00:52:42,470
When considering inheritance, it is the phenotype that is dominant or recessive, not the genotype.

401
00:52:42,470 --> 00:52:52,370
If a disease is dominant, a normal gene directs the production of normal protein, but the phenotype will be abnormal because of protein produced by the abnormal gene.

402
00:52:52,370 --> 00:53:04,833
A heterozygous carrier of a recessive disease may produce detectable levels of an abnormal gene product but have no features of the condition, because the phenotype is directed by the product of the normal co-gene.

403
00:53:04,833 --> 00:53:18,495
For example, erythrocytes from carriers of sickle-cell anemia contain approximately 30 percent hemoglobin S, but because the other 70 percent is hemoglobin A, these cells do not usually sickle in vivo.

404
00:53:18,495 --> 00:53:25,858
Heterogeneity Genetic heterogeneity explains how different genetic mechanisms may result in the same phenotype.

405
00:53:25,858 --> 00:53:33,595
Locus heterogeneity indicates that a specific disease phenotype can be caused by mutations in different genetic loci.

406
00:53:33,595 --> 00:53:39,133
It also explains why some diseases appear to follow more than one type of inheritance.

407
00:53:39,133 --> 00:53:52,145
For example, retinitis pigmentosa may develop following mutations in at least 35 different genes or loci and may result in autosomal dominant, autosomal recessive, or X-linked forms.

408
00:53:52,145 --> 00:53:59,295
Allelic heterogeneity describes how different mutations of the same gene may affect presentation of a particular

409
00:53:59,452 --> 00:54:01,052
disease.

410
00:54:01,002 --> 00:54:16,877
For example, although only one gene has been associated with cystic fibrosis—the cystic fibrosis conductance transmembrane regulator gene—more than 2000 mutations in this gene have been described and result in variable disease severity (Chaps.

411
00:54:16,877 --> 00:54:22,777
17, p. 342 and 54, p. 968).

412
00:54:22,777 --> 00:54:29,902
Phenotypic heterogeneity explains how different disease states can arise from different mutations in the same gene.

413
00:54:29,902 --> 00:54:47,264
As an example, mutations in the fibroblast growth factor receptor 3 (FGFR3) gene may result in several different skeletal disorders, including achondroplasia and thanatophoric dysplasia, both of which are discussed in Chapter 15 (p. 302).

414
00:54:47,264 --> 00:54:50,627
# Autosomal Dominant Inheritance.

415
00:54:50,627 --> 00:54:56,939
If only one copy of a gene pair determines the phenotype, that gene is considered to be dominant.

416
00:54:56,939 --> 00:55:02,602
Carriers have a 50-percent chance of passing on the affected gene with each conception.

417
00:55:02,602 --> 00:55:08,827
A gene with a dominant mutation generally specifies the phenotype in preference to the normal gene.

418
00:55:08,827 --> 00:55:15,914
That said, not all individuals will necessarily manifest an autosomal dominant condition the same way.

419
00:55:15,914 --> 00:55:24,589
Factors that affect the phenotype of an autosomal dominant condition include penetrance, expressivity, and presence of codominant genes.

420
00:55:24,589 --> 00:55:29,739
Penetrance This characteristic describes whether a dominant gene is expressed.

421
00:55:29,739 --> 00:55:35,889
A gene with recognizable phenotypic expression in all individuals has complete penetrance.

422
00:55:35,889 --> 00:55:41,102
Penetrance is incomplete if some carriers express the gene but others do not.

423
00:55:41,102 --> 00:55:46,414
A gene that is expressed in 80 percent of individuals is 80percent penetrant.

424
00:55:46,414 --> 00:55:53,164
Incomplete penetrance explains why some autosomal dominant diseases may appear to “skip” generations.

425
00:55:53,164 --> 00:56:01,539
Expressivity Individuals with the same autosomal dominant trait may manifest the condition differently, even within the same family.

426
00:56:01,539 --> 00:56:08,064
Genes with variable expressivity can produce disease manifestations that range from mild to severe.

427
00:56:08,064 --> 00:56:15,214
Examples include neurofibromatosis, tuberous sclerosis, and adult polycystic kidney disease.

428
00:56:15,214 --> 00:56:23,027
Codominant Genes If two different alleles in a gene pair are both expressed in the phenotype, they are considered to be codominant.

429
00:56:23,027 --> 00:56:31,627
Blood type, for example, is determined by expression of dominant A and B red-cell antigens that can be expressed simultaneously.

430
00:56:31,627 --> 00:56:37,439
Another example of codominance is the group of genes responsible for hemoglobin production.

431
00:56:37,439 --> 00:56:47,452
If one gene directs production of hemoglobin S and the other directs production of hemoglobin C, that individual will produce both S and C hemoglobin .

432
00:56:47,452 --> 00:56:55,277
Advanced Paternal Age Paternal age older than 40 is associated with increased risk for spontaneous genetic mutations.

433
00:56:55,277 --> 00:57:04,102
Spermatogonia undergo mitotic division every 16 days, and the many replications raise the risk for single base-pair mutations.

434
00:57:04,102 --> 00:57:12,002
As a result, offspring of older fathers are at risk for new autosomal dominant disorders and X-linked carrier states.

435
00:57:12,002 --> 00:57:16,652
Several in particular have been termed paternal age effect disorders .

436
00:57:16,652 --> 00:57:41,689
These include mutations in the fibroblast growth factor receptor 2 (FGFR2) gene, which may cause craniosynostosis syndromes such as Apert, Crouzon, and Pfeiffer syndromes; mutations in the FGFR3 gene, which may result in achondroplasia and thanatophoric dysplasia; and mutations in the RET proto-oncogene, which may cause multiple endocrine neoplasia syndromes .

437
00:57:41,689 --> 00:57:55,314
Using whole genome sequencing to study SNPs among offspring of older fathers, Kong and associates (2012) found an increase of approximately two mutations for each year of paternal age past 40.

438
00:57:55,314 --> 00:58:03,039
The absolute risk for any specific condition is low, because individual autosomal dominant disorders are uncommon.

439
00:58:03,039 --> 00:58:07,402
Thus, no screening or testing is specifically recommended.

440
00:58:07,402 --> 00:58:10,814
# Autosomal Recessive Inheritance.

441
00:58:10,814 --> 00:58:15,589
Recessive diseases develop only when both gene copies are abnormal.

442
00:58:15,589 --> 00:58:25,814
Unless carriers are screened for a specific disease, they usually are recognized only after the birth of an affected child or the diagnosis of an affected family member.

443
00:58:25,814 --> 00:58:34,127
If a couple has a child with an autosomal recessive disease, the recurrence risk is 25 percent for each subsequent pregnancy.

444
00:58:34,127 --> 00:58:39,602
Thus, 1/4 of offspring will be homozygous normal, 2/4 will be

445
00:58:39,772 --> 00:58:44,772
heterozygous carriers, and 1/4 will be homozygous abnormal.

446
00:58:44,722 --> 00:58:53,309
In other words, three of four children will be phenotypically normal, and 2/3 of phenotypically normal siblings are actually carriers.

447
00:58:53,309 --> 00:59:02,784
A heterozygous carrier of a recessive condition is only at risk to have affected children if her or his partner is heterozygous or homozygous for the disease.

448
00:59:02,784 --> 00:59:08,834
Genes for rare autosomal recessive conditions have low prevalence in the general population.

449
00:59:08,834 --> 00:59:17,597
Thus, the likelihood that a partner will be a gene carrier is small, unless there is consanguinity or the partner is a member of an at-risk group.

450
00:59:17,597 --> 00:59:22,784
Carrier screening is discussed in Chapter 17 (p. 342).

451
00:59:22,784 --> 00:59:35,759
Preimplantation genetic testing allows a blastocyst to be genetically evaluated prior to intrauterine transfer during in vitro fertilization and is described also in Chapter 17 (p. 348).

452
00:59:35,759 --> 00:59:47,934
Inborn Errors of Metabolism Most of these autosomal recessive diseases result from absence of a crucial enzyme, leading to incomplete metabolism of proteins, lipids, or carbohydrates.

453
00:59:47,934 --> 00:59:56,759
The metabolic intermediates that build up are toxic to various tissues and may result in intellectual disability or other abnormalities.

454
00:59:56,759 --> 00:59:58,847
Phenylketonuria.

455
00:59:58,847 --> 01:00:08,584
Also known as phenylalanine hydroxylase (PAH) deficiency, this autosomal recessive disease is caused by mutations in the PAH gene.

456
01:00:08,584 --> 01:00:16,197
PAH metabolizes phenylalanine to tyrosine, and homozygotes have diminished or absent enzyme activity.

457
01:00:16,197 --> 01:00:28,184
This leads to abnormally high levels of phenylalanine, which results in progressive intellectual impairment, autism, seizures, motor deficits, and neuropsychological abnormalities .

458
01:00:28,184 --> 01:00:40,234
Because phenylalanine competitively inhibits tyrosine hydroxylase, which is essential for melanin production, affected individuals also have hair, eye, and skin hypopigmentation.

459
01:00:40,234 --> 01:00:47,859
More than 600 PAH gene mutations have been characterized, and the carrier frequency varies by ethnicity.

460
01:00:47,859 --> 01:00:56,822
For those of Northern European origin, carrier frequency is 1 in 50, such that the disease affects up to 1 in 10,000 newborns .

461
01:00:56,822 --> 01:01:05,184
Prompt diagnosis and restriction of dietary phenylalanine beginning early in infancy are essential to prevent neurological damage.

462
01:01:05,184 --> 01:01:10,272
All states mandate newborn screening for phenylketonuria (PKU).

463
01:01:10,272 --> 01:01:19,809
Phenylalanine restriction alone would result in inadequate protein consumption, and phenylalanine-free amino acid–based supplementation is required.

464
01:01:19,809 --> 01:01:34,297
Maintenance of phenylalanine concentrations in the range of 2 to 6 mg/dL (120 to 360 μmol/L) is recommended from at least 3 months prior to pregnancy and continuing throughout pregnancy .

465
01:01:34,297 --> 01:01:39,297
Unfortunately, phenylalanine is actively transported to the fetus.

466
01:01:39,297 --> 01:01:55,584
Women with PKU whose phenylalanine levels remain above the recommended range during pregnancy are at risk to have otherwise normal (heterozygous) offspring who sustain significant in utero damage as a result of being exposed to toxic phenylalanine concentrations.

467
01:01:55,584 --> 01:02:08,984
Hyperphenylalaninemia raises the risk for miscarriage and for PKU embryopathy, which is characterized by intellectual disability, microcephaly, seizures, growth impairment, and cardiac anomalies.

468
01:02:08,984 --> 01:02:23,159
Among women on unrestricted diets, the risk to have a child with intellectual disability exceeds 90 percent, microcephaly develops in more than 70 percent, and as many as 1 in 6 children have cardiac defects.

469
01:02:23,159 --> 01:02:44,647
The Maternal Phenylketonuria Collaborative Study, which included 572 pregnancies followed more than 18 years, reported that maintenance of serum phenylalanine levels in the recommended range significantly reduced the fetal abnormality risk and resulted in normal childhood IQ scores (Koch, 2003; Platt, 2000).

470
01:02:44,647 --> 01:02:57,647
Approximately 25 to 50 percent of individuals with PKU experience a significant decline in phenylalanine levels when treated with the synthetic PAH cofactor tetrahydrobiopterin (sapropterin) .

471
01:02:57,647 --> 01:03:07,009
Based on registry data, sapropterin is considered a treatment option for those in whom phenylalanine levels remain elevated despite dietary therapy .

472
01:03:07,009 --> 01:03:14,047
Preconceptional counseling and consultation with providers from experienced PKU centers is recommended.

473
01:03:14,047 --> 01:03:19,247
Consanguinity In medical genetics, a union is consanguineous if

474
01:03:19,396 --> 01:03:22,946
between second cousins or closer relatives.

475
01:03:22,896 --> 01:03:31,258
Although uncommon in Western countries, the global estimate of consanguineous parentage approximates 10 percent of the population .

476
01:03:31,258 --> 01:03:38,958
More than 1 billion people are estimated to live in countries in which 20 to 50 percent of marriages are consanguineous .

477
01:03:38,958 --> 01:03:48,283
First-degree relatives share half of their genes, seconddegree relatives share a fourth, and third-degree relatives—first cousins— share one eighth.

478
01:03:48,283 --> 01:04:00,408
Because of the potential for shared deleterious genes, consanguinity confers an increased risk to have offspring with otherwise rare autosomal recessive diseases or multifactorial disorders.

479
01:04:00,408 --> 01:04:11,946
In populationbased series, first cousins have twice the risk for offspring with congenital abnormalities (Sheridan, 2013; Stoltenberg, 1997).

480
01:04:11,946 --> 01:04:16,596
Consanguinity is also associated with a greater rate of stillbirth .

481
01:04:16,596 --> 01:04:26,408
Because CMA performed using a SNP platform may identify consanguinity, it is important that preprocedural counseling include this possibility.

482
01:04:26,408 --> 01:04:35,071
Incest is defined as a sexual relationship between first-degree relatives such as parent-child or brother-sister and is universally illegal.

483
01:04:35,071 --> 01:04:39,996
Progeny of such unions carry the highest risk of abnormal outcomes.

484
01:04:39,996 --> 01:04:52,071
Older studies reported that up to 40 percent of offspring were abnormal as a result of recessive and multifactorial disorders (Baird, 1982; Freire-Maia, 1984).

485
01:04:52,071 --> 01:04:55,596
# X-Linked and Y-Linked Inheritance.

486
01:04:55,596 --> 01:04:58,783
Most X-linked diseases are recessive.

487
01:04:58,783 --> 01:05:06,346
Common examples include color blindness, hemophilia A and B, and Duchenne and Becker muscular dystrophy.

488
01:05:06,346 --> 01:05:16,346
A male with an X-linked recessive gene is usually affected by the disease it causes, because he lacks a second X chromosome to express the normal dominant gene.

489
01:05:16,346 --> 01:05:23,508
However, a male with an X-linked disease cannot have affected sons because they do not receive his X chromosome.

490
01:05:23,508 --> 01:05:34,696
When a woman carries a gene causing an X-linked recessive condition, each son has a 50-percent risk of being affected, and each daughter has a 50-percent chance of being a carrier.

491
01:05:34,696 --> 01:05:40,508
Women with an X-linked recessive gene are generally unaffected by the disease it causes.

492
01:05:40,508 --> 01:05:51,958
In some cases, however, the random inactivation of one X chromosome in each cell—termed lyonization—is skewed, and female carriers may have features of the condition.

493
01:05:51,958 --> 01:06:06,796
For example, approximately 10 percent of female carriers of hemophilia A will have factor VIII levels less than 30 percent of normal, and a similar proportion of female hemophilia B carriers have factor IX levels less than 30 percent.

494
01:06:06,796 --> 01:06:13,258
Levels below these thresholds confer a greater risk for abnormal bleeding when affected women give birth .

495
01:06:13,258 --> 01:06:20,158
Indeed, even with higher levels, carriers are reported to be at increased risk for bleeding complications .

496
01:06:20,158 --> 01:06:32,883
Female carriers of Duchenne or Becker muscular dystrophy carry an elevated risk for cardiomyopathy, and periodic evaluation for cardiac dysfunction and neuromuscular disorders is recommended .

497
01:06:32,883 --> 01:06:39,108
X-linked dominant disorders mainly affect females, because they tend to be lethal in males.

498
01:06:39,108 --> 01:06:44,408
Two examples are vitamin D–resistant rickets and incontinentia pigmenti.

499
01:06:44,408 --> 01:06:49,408
An important exception is fragile X syndrome, discussed subsequently.

500
01:06:49,408 --> 01:06:52,733
The prevalence of Y-linked disorders is low.

501
01:06:52,733 --> 01:07:01,671
The Y-chromosome carries genes important for sex determination and various cellular functions related to spermatogenesis and bone development.

502
01:07:01,671 --> 01:07:13,671
Deletion of genes on the long arm of Y results in severe spermatogenic defects, whereas genes at the tip of the short arm are critical for chromosomal pairing during meiosis and for fertility.

503
01:07:13,671 --> 01:07:16,771
# Mitochondrial Inheritance.

504
01:07:16,771 --> 01:07:23,821
Human cells contain hundreds of mitochondria, each with its own genome and associated replication system.

505
01:07:23,821 --> 01:07:28,171
Oocytes have approximately 100,000 mitochondria.

506
01:07:28,171 --> 01:07:33,621
Sperm have only about 100, and the latter are destroyed after fertilization.

507
01:07:33,621 --> 01:07:42,083
Each mitochondrion has multiple copies of a 16.5-kb circular DNA molecule that contains 37 genes.

508
01:07:42,083 --> 01:07:50,858
Mitochondrial DNA encodes peptides required for oxidative phosphorylation and encodes ribosomal and transfer RNAs.

509
01:07:50,858 --> 01:07:54,871
Mitochondria are inherited exclusively from the mother.

510
01:07:54,871 --> 01:07:58,471
Thus, although males and females both can

511
01:07:58,636 --> 01:08:04,923
be affected by a mitochondrial disorder, a male cannot transmit the condition to his offspring.

512
01:08:04,873 --> 01:08:13,848
When a cell replicates, mitochondrial DNA sorts randomly into each of the resulting cells, a process termed replicative segregation.

513
01:08:13,848 --> 01:08:22,348
As a consequence of replicative segregation, any mitochondrial mutation will be propagated randomly into the newly formed cells.

514
01:08:22,348 --> 01:08:32,873
Because each cell holds multiple copies of mitochondrial DNA, the mitochondrion may contain only normal or only abnormal DNA, termed homoplasmy.

515
01:08:32,873 --> 01:08:39,173
Alternatively, it may contain both normal and mutated DNA, namely heteroplasmy.

516
01:08:39,173 --> 01:08:49,411
If a heteroplasmic oocyte is fertilized, the relative proportion of mutated DNA may affect whether the individual manifests a given mitochondrial disease.

517
01:08:49,411 --> 01:08:57,561
It is not possible to predict the potential degree of heteroplasmy among offspring, and this poses challenges for genetic counseling.

518
01:08:57,561 --> 01:09:03,298
There are 33 mitochondrial diseases or conditions with known molecular bases .

519
01:09:03,298 --> 01:09:17,211
Examples include myoclonic epilepsy with ragged red fibers (MERRF), Leber optic atrophy, KearnsSayre syndrome, Leigh syndrome, and several forms of mitochondrial myopathy and cardiomyopathy.

520
01:09:17,211 --> 01:09:20,686
# DNA Triplet Repeat Expansion.

521
01:09:20,686 --> 01:09:28,798
Mendel’s first law is that genes are passed unchanged from parent to progeny, and barring new mutations, this is often true.

522
01:09:28,798 --> 01:09:37,248
Certain genes, however, contain a region of DNA trinucleotide repeats that can expand during parent-to-child transmission.

523
01:09:37,248 --> 01:09:45,948
The expansion can occur during male meiosis, as in Huntington disease, or during female meiosis, as in fragile X syndrome.

524
01:09:45,948 --> 01:09:58,373
Clinically, DNA triplet repeat expansion is manifested by anticipation—a phenomenon in which a disease may manifest at an earlier age or become more severe with each successive generation.

525
01:09:58,373 --> 01:10:03,973
Examples of DNA triplet repeat diseases are shown in Table 16-4.

526
01:10:03,973 --> 01:10:06,448
Table 16-4.

527
01:10:06,448 --> 01:10:15,048
Some Disorders Caused by DNA Triplet Repeat Expansion Fragile X Syndrome This is the most common inherited intellectual disability.

528
01:10:15,048 --> 01:10:22,811
Fragile X syndrome affects approximately 1 in 3600 males and 1 in 4000 to 6000 females .

529
01:10:22,811 --> 01:10:33,298
It is caused by expansion of a repeated trinucleotide DNA segment—cytosine-guanineguanine (CGG)—at chromosome Xq27.3.

530
01:10:33,298 --> 01:10:44,886
When the CGG repeat number reaches a critical size—the full mutation—the fragile X mental retardation 1 (FMR1) gene becomes methylated and inactivated.

531
01:10:44,886 --> 01:10:53,636
Inactivation halts expression of the FMR1 protein, which is abundant in nerve cells and necessary for normal cognitive development.

532
01:10:53,636 --> 01:11:04,198
Individuals with fragile X syndrome may have speech and language problems, attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder, and autism or autistic-like behaviors.

533
01:11:04,198 --> 01:11:12,086
Intellectual disability is generally more severe in males, in whom average IQ scores are 35 to 45 .

534
01:11:12,086 --> 01:11:24,023
The syndrome has characteristic phenotypic features—a narrow face with large jaw, prominent ears, connective tissue abnormalities, and macroorchidism in postpubertal males.

535
01:11:24,023 --> 01:11:31,348
Both the sex of the affected individual and the number of CGG repeats determine the degree of clinical impairment.

536
01:11:31,348 --> 01:11:45,473
Clinically, four groups have been described : • Full mutation—more than 200 repeats • Premutation—55 to 200 repeats • Intermediate—45 to 54 repeats • Unaffected—fewer than 45 repeats.

537
01:11:45,473 --> 01:11:50,023
Full mutations are expressed in all males and many females.

538
01:11:50,023 --> 01:11:57,748
Males with full mutations typically have significant cognitive and behavioral abnormalities and phenotypic features.

539
01:11:57,748 --> 01:12:06,211
In females, random X chromosome inactivation results in variable expression, and the disability may be much less severe.

540
01:12:06,211 --> 01:12:12,898
With rare exception, the parent of origin of repeat expansion that leads to a full mutation is female .

541
01:12:12,898 --> 01:12:16,586
Counseling for a premutation is more complex.

542
01:12:16,586 --> 01:12:31,898
The woman with a fragile X premutation may have offspring with the full mutation, and the risk ranges from ≤5 percent with fewer than 70 CGG repeats to >95 percent with 100 to 200 CGG repeats .

543
01:12:31,898 --> 01:12:39,536
Expansion is extremely unlikely in a male premutation carrier, but all of his daughters will carry the premutation.

544
01:12:39,536 --> 01:12:49,173
Among women with no risk factors, approximately 1 in 250 carries a fragile X premutation, and in those with a family history of

545
01:12:49,324 --> 01:12:53,961
intellectual disability, the risk approximates 1 in 90 .

546
01:12:53,911 --> 01:13:00,186
Fragile X premutation carriers may themselves experience significant health consequences.

547
01:13:00,186 --> 01:13:12,774
Males with a premutation are at risk for the fragile X tremor ataxia syndrome (FXTAS), which is characterized by memory loss, executive function deficits, anxiety, and dementia .

548
01:13:12,774 --> 01:13:22,711
Females are at decreased risk for FXTAS, but they have a 20-percent risk for fragile X-associated primary ovarian insufficiency (POI).

549
01:13:22,711 --> 01:13:35,049
Carrier screening is recommended for women with a family history of fragile X syndrome, for those with unexplained intellectual disability, developmental delay, or autism, and for women with POI .

550
01:13:35,049 --> 01:13:41,174
Prenatal diagnosis can be accomplished by amniocentesis or chorionic villus sampling.

551
01:13:41,174 --> 01:13:52,161
Either type of specimen can be used to evaluate the CGG repeat number, but chorionic villus sampling may not accurately determine FMR1 gene methylation status.

552
01:13:52,161 --> 01:13:54,299
# Imprinting.

553
01:13:54,299 --> 01:14:00,749
Usually, one copy of each gene is inherited from each parent, and both copies are expressed.

554
01:14:00,749 --> 01:14:06,986
Imprinting refers to the situation in which gene expression varies according to the parent of origin.

555
01:14:06,986 --> 01:14:14,911
With maternal imprinting, a gene inherited from the mother is transcriptionally silent and the gene inherited from the father is active.

556
01:14:14,911 --> 01:14:17,686
Paternal imprinting is the opposite.

557
01:14:17,686 --> 01:14:28,774
This phenomenon explains the phenotypic differences that occur in triploid pregnancies according to whether the extra chromosomal set is maternal or paternal (p. 313).

558
01:14:28,774 --> 01:14:38,899
Imprinting affects gene expression by epigenetic control, that is, it modifies the phenotype without altering the underlying genetic structure or genotype.

559
01:14:38,899 --> 01:14:52,374
Importantly, the effect may be reversed in a subsequent generation, because a female who inherits an imprinted gene from her father will pass it in her oocytes with a maternal—rather than paternal—imprint, and vice versa.

560
01:14:52,374 --> 01:14:59,249
An example of imprinting can be found in two very different diseases associated with the same region of DNA.

561
01:14:59,249 --> 01:15:09,386
Prader-Willi syndrome is characterized by pronounced hyperphagia with obesity, along with short stature, small hands and feet, and mild mental retardation.

562
01:15:09,386 --> 01:15:21,849
In more than 70 percent of cases, Prader-Willi is caused by microdeletion or disruption of a DNA segment on the paternal chromosome 15, 15q11.2-q13.

563
01:15:21,849 --> 01:15:29,811
The remaining cases are due to maternal uniparental disomy or due to maternal gene imprinting with the paternal gene inactivated.

564
01:15:29,811 --> 01:15:43,974
In contrast, individuals with Angelman syndrome have normal stature and weight but severe intellectual disability, absent speech, seizures, ataxia, jerky arm movements, and paroxysms of inappropriate laughter.

565
01:15:43,974 --> 01:15:55,749
In approximately 70 percent of cases, Angelman syndrome is caused by microdeletion for the 15q11.2-q13 DNA segment on the mother’s chromosome 15.

566
01:15:55,749 --> 01:16:03,111
This segment contains a gene that codes for ubiquitin protein ligase E3A, an important neural protein.

567
01:16:03,111 --> 01:16:13,336
Paternal gene imprinting defects—with maternal genes inactivated—account for 2 to 3 percent, and 2 percent result from paternal uniparental disomy.

568
01:16:13,336 --> 01:16:18,549
Table 16-5 lists selected diseases that can involve imprinting.

569
01:16:18,549 --> 01:16:21,124
Table 16-5.

570
01:16:21,124 --> 01:16:25,961
Some Disorders That Can Involve Imprinting # Uniparental Disomy.

571
01:16:25,961 --> 01:16:31,336
This occurs when both members of a chromosome pair are inherited from the same parent.

572
01:16:31,336 --> 01:16:43,949
Uniparental disomy usually has no clinical consequences, because gene expression is not generally affected by the parent of origin and because both copies of a chromosome pair are not usually identical.

573
01:16:43,949 --> 01:16:57,974
However, if chromosomes 6, 7, 11, 14, or 15 are involved, offspring are at increased risk for an abnormality because of parent-of-origin differences in gene expression, that is, because of imprinting .

574
01:16:57,974 --> 01:17:06,949
Several genetic mechanisms may cause uniparental disomy, the most common of which is trisomic rescue, shown in Figure 16-9.

575
01:17:06,949 --> 01:17:19,111
After a nondisjunction event produces a trisomic conceptus, one of the three homologues may be lost, resulting in uniparental disomy for that chromosome in approximately one third of cases.

576
01:17:19,111 --> 01:17:21,624
Figure 16-9.

577
01:17:21,624 --> 01:17:26,386
Mechanism of uniparental disomy arising from trisomic “rescue.

578
01:17:26,386 --> 01:17:31,774
A. In normal meiosis, one member of each pair of homologous chromosomes

579
01:17:31,924 --> 01:17:34,549
is inherited from each parent.

580
01:17:34,499 --> 01:17:40,974
B. If nondisjunction results in a trisomic conceptus, one homologue is sometimes lost.

581
01:17:40,974 --> 01:17:46,174
In a third of cases, loss of one homologue leads to uniparental disomy.

582
01:17:46,174 --> 01:17:53,849
Uniparental isodisomy occurs when an individual receives two identical copies of one chromosome from the same parent.

583
01:17:53,849 --> 01:18:07,499
This mechanism has explained cases of cystic fibrosis in which only one parent is a carrier but the fetus inherits two copies of the same gene mutation (Spence, 1988; Spotila, 1992).

584
01:18:07,499 --> 01:18:10,836
# Multifactorial Inheritance.

585
01:18:10,836 --> 01:18:20,011
Traits or diseases that are determined by the combination of multiple genes and environmental factors are considered to have multifactorial inheritance .

586
01:18:20,011 --> 01:18:25,049
Polygenic traits are determined by the combined effects of more than one gene.

587
01:18:25,049 --> 01:18:31,449
Many congenital and acquired conditions and common traits display multifactorial inheritance.

588
01:18:31,449 --> 01:18:44,124
Examples include malformations such as neural-tube defects, cardiac defects, and facial clefts; diseases such as diabetes and atherosclerosis; and traits such as head size or height.

589
01:18:44,124 --> 01:18:50,886
Multifactorial abnormalities tend to recur in families, but not according to a mendelian pattern.

590
01:18:50,886 --> 01:18:59,899
If a couple has had 1 child with a multifactorial birth defect, the risk that another child will be affected is empirically 3 to 5 percent.

591
01:18:59,899 --> 01:19:05,674
This risk declines exponentially with successively more distant family relationships.

592
01:19:05,674 --> 01:19:08,211
Table 16-6.

593
01:19:08,211 --> 01:19:17,399
Characteristics of Multifactorial Diseases Multifactorial traits that have a normal distribution in the population are termed continuously variable.

594
01:19:17,399 --> 01:19:24,486
A measurement that is more than two standard deviations above or below the population mean may be considered abnormal.

595
01:19:24,486 --> 01:19:33,999
Continuously variable traits tend to be less extreme in the offspring of affected individuals, because of the statistical principle of regression to the mean.

596
01:19:33,999 --> 01:19:39,599
Threshold Traits Some multifactorial traits do not appear until a threshold is exceeded.

597
01:19:39,599 --> 01:19:52,874
Genetic and environmental factors that create propensity or liability for the trait are themselves normally distributed, and only individuals at extremes of the distribution exceed the threshold and exhibit the trait or defect.

598
01:19:52,874 --> 01:19:57,349
The phenotypic abnormality is thus an all-or-none phenomenon.

599
01:19:57,349 --> 01:20:01,924
Certain threshold traits have a clear male or female predominance.

600
01:20:01,924 --> 01:20:09,849
If an individual of the less affected gender has the characteristic or defect, the risk is greater in his or her offspring .

601
01:20:09,849 --> 01:20:16,011
An example is pyloric stenosis, which is approximately four times more common in males .

602
01:20:16,011 --> 01:20:30,274
A female with pyloric stenosis has likely inherited more predisposing genetic factors than are necessary to produce the defect in a male, and the recurrence risk for her children or siblings is thus higher than the expected 3 to 5 percent.

603
01:20:30,274 --> 01:20:40,299
Her brothers and sons would have the highest liability because they not only will inherit more than the usual number of predisposing genes but also are the more susceptible gender.

604
01:20:40,299 --> 01:20:42,786
Figure 16-10.

605
01:20:42,786 --> 01:20:49,886
Schematic example of a threshold trait, such as pyloric stenosis, which has a predilection for males.

606
01:20:49,886 --> 01:20:57,199
Each gender is normally distributed, but at the same threshold, more males than females will develop the condition.

607
01:20:57,199 --> 01:21:02,274
The recurrence risk for threshold traits is also greater if the defect is severe.

608
01:21:02,274 --> 01:21:14,736
For example, the recurrence risk after the birth of a child with bilateral cleft lip and palate approximates 8 percent, but it is only about 4 percent following a child with unilateral cleft lip alone.

609
01:21:14,736 --> 01:21:23,424
Cardiac Defects Structural cardiac anomalies are the most common birth defects, with a birth prevalence of 8 cases per 1000.

610
01:21:23,424 --> 01:21:32,911
The risk of having a child with a cardiac anomaly approximates 5 to 6 percent if the mother has the defect and 2 to 3 percent if the father is affected .

611
01:21:32,911 --> 01:21:50,474
Selected left-sided lesions, including hypoplastic left heart syndrome, coarctation of the aorta, and bicuspid aortic valve, may have recurrence risks four- to sixfold higher (Lin, 1988; Lupton, 2002; Nora, 1988).

612
01:21:50,474 --> 01:21:58,636
Observed recurrence risks for specific cardiac malformations are listed in Table 52-4 (p. 920).

613
01:21:58,636 --> 01:22:07,049
Neural-tube Defects Risk for developing a neural-tube defect (NTD) is influenced by family history, hyperthermia,

614
01:22:07,204 --> 01:22:13,391
hyperglycemia, teratogen exposure, obesity, ethnicity, and fetal gender.

615
01:22:13,341 --> 01:22:23,416
More than 50 years ago, Hibbard and Smithells (1965) postulated that abnormal folate metabolism was responsible for many NTDs.

616
01:22:23,416 --> 01:22:36,329
However, most NTD cases do not occur in the setting of maternal folic acid deficiency, and it has become clear that the gene-nutrient interactions underlying folate-responsive NTDs are complex.

617
01:22:36,329 --> 01:22:54,441
For a woman with a prior affected child, the 3- to 5-percent recurrence risk declines by at least 70 percent with 4 mg/d of periconceptional oral folic acid supplementation (Grosse, 2007; MRC Vitamin Study Research Group, 1991).

618
01:22:54,441 --> 01:23:13,666
Periconceptional folic acid supplementation also appears to ameliorate the fetal NTD risk in women with pregestational diabetes and in those with exposure to antiepileptic medications, fever during embryogenesis, and obesity (Kerr, 2017; Petersen, 2019).

619
01:23:13,666 --> 01:23:31,316
Sonographic features of NTDs are described in Chapter 15 (p. 276), their prevention with folic acid is discussed in Chapter 9 (p. 168), and fetal therapy for myelomeningocele is reviewed in Chapter 19 (p. 372).

620
01:23:31,316 --> 01:23:33,304
Genetic Tests.

621
01:23:33,304 --> 01:23:40,191
All pregnant women should have the option of prenatal aneuploidy screening and prenatal genetic diagnosis .

622
01:23:40,191 --> 01:23:46,354
Screening for fetal aneuploidy may be either serum analyte-based or cell-free DNA-based.

623
01:23:46,354 --> 01:23:55,129
Prenatal genetic carrier screening tests for cystic fibrosis and spinal muscular atrophy carrier status also are routinely offered.

624
01:23:55,129 --> 01:24:00,029
Additional carrier screening tests are offered to at-risk individuals.

625
01:24:00,029 --> 01:24:04,529
Expanded genetic carrier screening panels also are available.

626
01:24:04,529 --> 01:24:14,566
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists considers ethnicspecific, panethnic, and expanded carrier screening acceptable strategies .

627
01:24:14,566 --> 01:24:19,991
These screening tests are discussed in Chapter 17 (p. 342).

628
01:24:19,991 --> 01:24:33,666
For prenatal genetic diagnosis, the most commonly used tests are chromosomal microarray analysis (CMA), cytogenetic analysis (karyotyping), and fluorescence in situ hybridization (FISH).

629
01:24:33,666 --> 01:24:38,479
Testing may be performed on amnionic fluid or chorionic villi.

630
01:24:38,479 --> 01:24:46,904
In selected circumstances, whole genome or whole exome sequencing may be considered, but these are not recommended for routine use.

631
01:24:46,904 --> 01:25:00,341
To diagnose a specific disease whose genetic basis is known, DNA-based tests are often employed, typically using polymerase chain reaction (PCR) for rapid amplification of DNA sequences.

632
01:25:00,341 --> 01:25:03,366
# Cytogenetic Analysis.

633
01:25:03,366 --> 01:25:12,291
Also known as karyotyping, cytogenetic analysis tests for numerical chromosomal abnormalities—aneuploidy or polyploidy.

634
01:25:12,291 --> 01:25:19,929
It can also identify balanced or unbalanced structural rearrangements of at least 5 to 10 megabases in size.

635
01:25:19,929 --> 01:25:24,879
Karyotyping has diagnostic accuracy exceeding 99 percent.

636
01:25:24,879 --> 01:25:32,566
Any tissue containing dividing cells or cells that can be stimulated to divide is suitable for cytogenetic analysis.

637
01:25:32,566 --> 01:25:39,579
The dividing cells are arrested in metaphase, and their chromosomes are stained to reveal light and dark bands.

638
01:25:39,579 --> 01:25:46,491
The most commonly used technique is Giemsa staining, which yields the Gbands shown in Figure 16-3.

639
01:25:46,491 --> 01:25:55,379
Each chromosome has a unique banding pattern that permits its identification and detection of deleted, duplicated, or rearranged segments.

640
01:25:55,379 --> 01:26:00,791
The accuracy of cytogenetic analysis rises with the number of bands produced.

641
01:26:00,791 --> 01:26:12,191
Metaphase banding routinely yields 450 to 550 visible bands per haploid chromosome set, whereas prophase banding generally yields 850 bands.

642
01:26:12,191 --> 01:26:19,941
Only dividing cells can be evaluated, so the growth of cultured cells affects the rapidity with which results are obtained.

643
01:26:19,941 --> 01:26:30,404
Amnionic fluid, which contains amniocytes, epithelial cells, and gastrointestinal mucosal cells, will usually yield results in 7 to 10 days .

644
01:26:30,404 --> 01:26:36,491
Fetal blood cells may provide results in 36 to 48 hours but are rarely needed.

645
01:26:36,491 --> 01:26:43,604
If fetal skin fibroblasts are evaluated postmortem, stimulation of cell growth can be more difficult.

646
01:26:43,604 --> 01:26:50,679
Cytogenetic analysis may take 2 to 3 weeks in such cases and has largely been replaced by CMA .

647
01:26:50,679 --> 01:26:54,291
# Fluorescence In Situ Hybridization.

648
01:26:54,291 --> 01:27:04,654
This technique is most commonly used for rapid identification of a specific chromosomal abnormality, such as trisomy 21, 18, or 13.

649
01:27:04,654 --> 01:27:07,966
Because of its 1- to 2-day turnaround

650
01:27:08,116 --> 01:27:14,391
time, FISH is typically selected for cases in which findings may alter pregnancy management.

651
01:27:14,341 --> 01:27:25,591
It may also be used to verify a suspected deletion syndrome, such as the 22q11.2 microdeletion (p. 315), although CMA is preferred.

652
01:27:25,591 --> 01:27:37,941
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2018) recommends that decision-making based on FISH should incorporate clinical information consistent with the suspected diagnosis.

653
01:27:37,941 --> 01:27:48,828
Supportive data may be an abnormal aneuploidy screening test result, a sonographic finding, or a confirmatory diagnostic test such as karyotyping or CMA.

654
01:27:48,828 --> 01:27:57,053
To perform FISH, cells are fixed onto a glass slide, and fluorescentlabeled probes are hybridized to the fixed chromosomes .

655
01:27:57,053 --> 01:28:03,703
Each probe is a DNA sequence that is complementary to a chromosome region or gene being investigated.

656
01:28:03,703 --> 01:28:10,728
If the DNA sequence is present, hybridization is detected as a bright signal visible by microscopy.

657
01:28:10,728 --> 01:28:16,316
The number of signals indicates the number of chromosomes or genes of that type in the cell.

658
01:28:16,316 --> 01:28:24,028
FISH does not provide information on the entire chromosomal complement but merely the chromosomal or gene region of interest.

659
01:28:24,028 --> 01:28:34,391
Figure 16-12 shows an example of interphase FISH using α-satellite probes for chromosomes 18, X, and Y to confirm trisomy 18.

660
01:28:34,391 --> 01:28:36,953
Figure 16-11.

661
01:28:36,953 --> 01:28:41,166
Steps in fluorescence in situ hybridization (FISH).

662
01:28:41,166 --> 01:28:43,716
Figure 16-12.

663
01:28:43,716 --> 01:28:52,303
Interphase fluorescence in situ hybridization (FISH) using α-satellite probes for chromosomes 18, X, and Y.

664
01:28:52,303 --> 01:29:01,816
In this case, the three light blue signals, two green signals, and absence of red signals indicate that this is a female fetus with trisomy 18.

665
01:29:01,816 --> 01:29:05,553
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Frederick Elder.

666
01:29:05,553 --> 01:29:09,103
# Chromosomal Microarray Analysis.

667
01:29:09,103 --> 01:29:19,153
This test is 100 times more sensitive than karyotyping and detects microduplications and microdeletions as small as 50 to 100 kilobases.

668
01:29:19,153 --> 01:29:25,941
Direct CMA, which is performed on uncultured cells, can yield results in 3 to 7 days.

669
01:29:25,941 --> 01:29:30,928
If cultured cells are required, results may take 10 to 14 days .

670
01:29:30,928 --> 01:29:40,078
Microarrays use either a comparative genomic hybridization (CGH) platform, a SNP platform, or a combination of the two.

671
01:29:40,078 --> 01:29:46,178
The CGH microarray platform compares specimen DNA with a normal control sample.

672
01:29:46,178 --> 01:29:49,428
Shown in Figure 16-13.

673
01:29:49,428 --> 01:29:56,028
the CGH chip contains reference DNA fragments of known sequence—oligonucleotides.

674
01:29:56,028 --> 01:30:05,628
Fetal DNA from the amniocentesis or chorionic villus sampling specimen is labeled with a fluorescent dye and then hybridized to the DNA on the chip.

675
01:30:05,628 --> 01:30:11,578
Normal control DNA is labeled with a different probe and also hybridized to the same chip.

676
01:30:11,578 --> 01:30:16,228
The intensity of the fluorescent signals from the two samples is compared.

677
01:30:16,228 --> 01:30:24,353
With a SNP array, the chip contains known DNA sequence variants, that is, single-nucleotide polymorphisms.

678
01:30:24,353 --> 01:30:32,291
When fetal DNA is labeled and hybridized to the chip, the fluorescent signal intensity indicates copy number variation.

679
01:30:32,291 --> 01:30:35,028
Figure 16-13.

680
01:30:35,028 --> 01:30:38,053
Chromosomal microarray analysis.

681
01:30:38,053 --> 01:30:41,241
A. Actual microarray chip size.

682
01:30:41,241 --> 01:30:45,366
B. Each chip contains thousands of cells (squares).

683
01:30:45,366 --> 01:30:47,253
C & D.

684
01:30:47,253 --> 01:30:54,803
Each cell contains thousands of identical oligonucleotides on its surface and is unique in its nucleotide content.

685
01:30:54,803 --> 01:31:01,441
E. During genetic analysis, a mixture containing tagged fetal DNA is presented to the chip.

686
01:31:01,441 --> 01:31:04,703
Complementary DNA sequences bind.

687
01:31:04,703 --> 01:31:12,253
F. If a laser is shined on the chip, DNA sequences that have bound will glow thus identifying a matching sequence.

688
01:31:12,253 --> 01:31:17,353
Modified with permission from Doody KJ: Treatment of the infertile couple.

689
01:31:17,353 --> 01:31:25,153
In Hoffman BL, Schorge JO, Schaffer JI, et al (eds): Williams Gynecology, 2nd ed.

690
01:31:25,153 --> 01:31:29,391
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2012.

691
01:31:29,391 --> 01:31:37,603
Both types of platforms detect aneuploidy, unbalanced translocations, and microdeletions and microduplications.

692
01:31:37,603 --> 01:31:44,703
In addition, SNP arrays are able to identify triploidy and can detect absence of heterozygosity.

693
01:31:44,703 --> 01:31:51,653
The latter can occur with uniparental disomy, in which both copies of a chromosome are inherited from one parent.

694
01:31:51,653 --> 01:32:02,866
Absence of heterozygosity may also occur with consanguinity, and counseling prior to performance of an SNP array should include this possibility (p. 321).

695
01:32:02,866 --> 01:32:09,491
Importantly, neither type of array platform currently detects balanced chromosomal rearrangements.

696
01:32:09,491 --> 01:32:11,328
For this reason,

697
01:32:11,500 --> 01:32:17,262
couples with recurrent pregnancy loss should be offered karyotyping as the first-line test .

698
01:32:17,212 --> 01:32:30,112
In addition to identifying pathogenic copy number variants, CMA may detect variants that are unable to be classified as benign or pathologic and thus are considered to be of uncertain clinical significance.

699
01:32:30,112 --> 01:32:44,037
In recent series, these variants of uncertain significance are identified in approximately 1 percent of prenatal specimens (Brady, 2014; Chong, 2019; Wang, 2018).

700
01:32:44,037 --> 01:32:53,000
Not unexpectedly, variants of uncertain significance may be a source of distress to families, even with comprehensive pretest counseling.

701
01:32:53,000 --> 01:33:06,512
Clinical Applications In pregnancies at increased risk for autosomal trisomy based on aneuploidy screening results, karyotyping or FISH plus karyotyping should be offered, and CMA should be made available .

702
01:33:06,512 --> 01:33:10,600
Clinicians are increasingly offering CMA in this setting.

703
01:33:10,600 --> 01:33:29,800
If the karyotype is normal, CMA has identified clinically relevant copy number variants in approximately 6.5 percent of pregnancies with fetal abnormalities and in 1 to 2 percent of those without obvious fetal abnormality (Callaway, 2013; Chong, 2019).

704
01:33:29,800 --> 01:33:44,675
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2018) and the Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine (2016) recommend CMA as the first-tier test when fetal structural abnormalities are identified.

705
01:33:44,675 --> 01:34:01,750
If a particular anomaly that strongly suggests a specific aneuploidy is identified, such as an endocardial cushion defect (trisomy 21) or alobar holoprosencephaly (trisomy 13), then karyotyping or FISH may be offered as the initial test.

706
01:34:01,750 --> 01:34:09,400
Genetic counseling should include information regarding the benefits and limitations of both CMA and karyotyping.

707
01:34:09,400 --> 01:34:14,050
Each should be made available to women who elect prenatal diagnosis .

708
01:34:14,050 --> 01:34:25,475
CMA may identify instances of autosomal dominant genetic disorders that have not yet manifested in an affected parent, and it may also identify instances of nonpaternity.

709
01:34:25,475 --> 01:34:36,862
For stillbirth evaluation, CMA is significantly more likely than standard karyotyping to provide a genetic diagnosis, in part because it does not require dividing cells.

710
01:34:36,862 --> 01:34:49,912
The Stillbirth Collaborative Research Network found that when karyotyping was uninformative, approximately 6 percent of cases had either aneuploidy or a pathogenic copy number variant identified with CMA .

711
01:34:49,912 --> 01:35:04,450
In a recent metaanalysis of more than 900 stillbirths with normal karyotype, CMA identified pathogenic copy number variants in 6 percent with structural abnormalities and 3 percent with no abnormalities evident .

712
01:35:04,450 --> 01:35:10,612
Overall, CMA yielded results 15 percent more often than karyotyping alone.

713
01:35:10,612 --> 01:35:14,200
# Whole Genome and Whole Exome Sequencing.

714
01:35:14,200 --> 01:35:20,687
Most fetuses with structural abnormalities have a normal karyotype and a normal CMA result.

715
01:35:20,687 --> 01:35:26,937
Whole genome sequencing (WGS) is a technique for analyzing the entire genome.

716
01:35:26,937 --> 01:35:36,087
Whole exome sequencing (WES) analyzes only the DNA coding regions, which account for approximately 1.5 percent of the genome.

717
01:35:36,087 --> 01:35:45,275
These next-generation sequencing tools are increasingly used postnatally to evaluate suspected genetic syndromes and intellectual disability.

718
01:35:45,275 --> 01:35:57,412
Several prospective, multicenter series have evaluated WES for pregnancies in which fetuses have structural abnormalities, no karyotypic evidence of aneuploidy, and normal CMA.

719
01:35:57,412 --> 01:36:07,612
WES identified genetic abnormalities in approximately 10 percent of such cases (Lord, 2019; Petrovski, 2019).

720
01:36:07,612 --> 01:36:14,025
Utility was higher in cases with cardiac, skeletal, or multiorgan system anomalies.

721
01:36:14,025 --> 01:36:19,862
Importantly, WGS and WES have significant limitations in their current form.

722
01:36:19,862 --> 01:36:35,837
These include prohibitively long turnaround times, high costs, and increased rates of false-positive and false-negative results and variants of uncertain significance (American College of Medical Genetics, 2012; Atwal, 2014).

723
01:36:35,837 --> 01:36:49,712
In addition, because sequencing is generally performed on the fetus and parents simultaneously, one parent may be identified or suspected to have an unrelated but medically actionable finding, which further complicates counseling .

724
01:36:49,712 --> 01:36:57,662
As a result, the clinical utility of this promising technology for prenatal cases is currently limited, and it is not

725
01:36:57,820 --> 01:37:00,895
recommended for routine clinical use.

726
01:37:00,845 --> 01:37:04,670
# Fetal DNA in the Maternal Circulation.

727
01:37:04,670 --> 01:37:11,820
Fetal cells are present in maternal blood at a very low concentration, only 2 to 6 cells per milliliter .

728
01:37:11,820 --> 01:37:18,120
Intact fetal cells sometimes persist in the maternal circulation for decades following delivery.

729
01:37:18,120 --> 01:37:23,232
Persistent fetal cells may engraft in the mother, causing microchimerism .

730
01:37:23,232 --> 01:37:32,570
This has been implicated in maternal autoimmune diseases such as scleroderma, systemic lupus erythematosus, and Hashimoto thyroiditis .

731
01:37:32,570 --> 01:37:47,145
From the standpoint of prenatal diagnosis, use of intact fetal cells from maternal blood is limited by low cell concentration, cell persistence into successive pregnancies, and difficulties in distinguishing fetal and maternal cells.

732
01:37:47,145 --> 01:37:50,895
Cell-free DNA overcomes these limitations.

733
01:37:50,895 --> 01:38:04,682
Cell-free DNA Also known as cell-free fetal DNA or noninvasive prenatal screening (NIPS), this screening test uses DNA fragments derived from maternal cells and from apoptotic placental trophoblast cells.

734
01:38:04,682 --> 01:38:07,007
Fetal” is thus a misnomer.

735
01:38:07,007 --> 01:38:13,295
Cell-free DNA is reliably detected in maternal blood after 9 to 10 weeks’ gestation .

736
01:38:13,295 --> 01:38:24,920
The proportion of total cell-free DNA that is placental is called the fetal fraction, and it accounts for approximately 10 percent of the total circulating cell-free DNA in maternal plasma.

737
01:38:24,920 --> 01:38:30,870
Unlike intact fetal cells, cell-free DNA is cleared within minutes from maternal blood.

738
01:38:30,870 --> 01:38:37,820
Current clinical applications of cell-free DNA are aneuploidy screening and Rh D genotyping .

739
01:38:37,820 --> 01:38:54,270
Commercial laboratories have also offered two other applications of cell-free DNA: (1) screening for selected microdeletion and microduplication syndromes and (2) genome-wide screening for deletions and duplications larger than 7 megabase pairs.

740
01:38:54,270 --> 01:39:01,932
Currently, prospective data on these promising applications are limited, and neither is recommended for routine screening .

741
01:39:01,932 --> 01:39:04,607
Figure 16-14.

742
01:39:04,607 --> 01:39:07,132
Cell-free DNA screening.

743
01:39:07,132 --> 01:39:12,245
These DNA fragments are derived from apoptotic trophoblast cells.

744
01:39:12,245 --> 01:39:26,695
Screening for autosomal trisomies and sex chromosomal aneuploidies may be performed using whole-genome sequencing, chromosome-selective or -targeted sequencing, and analysis of single-nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs).

745
01:39:26,695 --> 01:39:37,957
Real-time quantitative polymerase chain reaction (PCR) also may be used to target specific regions or sequences, a technique used for Rh D genotyping.

746
01:39:37,957 --> 01:39:42,582
Additional applications for cell-free DNA are on the horizon.

747
01:39:42,582 --> 01:40:05,820
In research settings, numerous single-gene disorders have been detected and include skeletal dysplasias, hemoglobinopathies, cystic fibrosis, congenital adrenal hyperplasia, hemophilia, muscular dystrophy, myotonic dystrophy, and spinal muscular atrophy (Camunas-Soler, 2018; Jenkins, 2018; Zhang, 2019).

748
01:40:05,820 --> 01:40:07,970
Aneuploidy Screening.

749
01:40:07,970 --> 01:40:15,557
Several different assay platforms are used to screen for fetal autosomal trisomies and sex chromosomal aneuploidies.

750
01:40:15,557 --> 01:40:31,357
These include massively parallel sequencing, which is a WGS technique; chromosome-selective or targeted sequencing; and analysis of SNPs (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2018, 2020c).

751
01:40:31,357 --> 01:40:43,495
By simultaneously sequencing millions of chromosome-specific DNA fragments, investigators can identify whether the proportion or ratio of fragments from one chromosome is higher than expected.

752
01:40:43,495 --> 01:40:51,457
Thus, samples from women with a Down syndrome fetus will have a larger proportion of DNA sequences from chromosome 21.

753
01:40:51,457 --> 01:40:55,645
The screening performance of cell-free DNA is excellent.

754
01:40:55,645 --> 01:41:11,920
In a metaanalysis of 35 studies of largely high-risk pregnancies, the pooled sensitivity to detect Down syndrome was 99.7 percent, and to identify trisomies 18 and 13, 98 and 99 percent, respectively .

755
01:41:11,920 --> 01:41:20,720
For each, the specificity was at least 99.9 percent, such that the cumulative falsepositive rate was below 1 percent.

756
01:41:20,720 --> 01:41:32,832
Aneuploidy screening reports commonly include information about fetal sex, which may be clinically useful if the fetus is at risk for an X-linked disorder or for congenital adrenal hyperplasia .

757
01:41:32,832 --> 01:41:45,820
In a metaanalysis, the sensitivity of cell-free DNA testing for fetal sex determination was reported to be 95 percent between 7 and 12 weeks’ gestation and 99 percent after 20 weeks .

758
01:41:45,988 --> 01:41:52,400
Causes of false-positive and false-negative screening results are listed in Table 16-7.

759
01:41:52,350 --> 01:41:59,950
As with any other screening test, diagnostic confirmation should be performed before irreversible medical intervention.

760
01:41:59,950 --> 01:42:05,375
Cell-free DNA screens do not yield a result in 2 to 4 percent of cases.

761
01:42:05,375 --> 01:42:16,775
This may be due to assay failure, high assay variance, or low fetal fraction (Norton, 2012; Pergament, 2014; Quezada, 2015).

762
01:42:16,775 --> 01:42:22,138
Importantly, such pregnancies carry a greater risk for fetal aneuploidy.

763
01:42:22,138 --> 01:42:44,450
In addition, results may not reflect the fetal DNA complement but rather may indicate confined placental mosaicism, early demise of an aneuploid co-twin, maternal mosaicism, or rarely, occult maternal malignancy (Bianchi, 2015; Curnow, 2015; Grati, 2014b; Wang, 2014).

764
01:42:44,450 --> 01:42:50,588
Recommendations for counseling are discussed in Chapter 17 (p. 336).

765
01:42:50,588 --> 01:42:53,125
Table 16-7.

766
01:42:53,125 --> 01:43:00,288
Selected Etiologies Creating Inaccurate Cell-free DNA Results Rh D Genotype Evaluation.

767
01:43:00,288 --> 01:43:05,613
Many fetuses of Rh D-negative women are also Rh D-negative.

768
01:43:05,613 --> 01:43:10,913
In a predominantly white population, this prevalence approaches 40 percent.

769
01:43:10,913 --> 01:43:22,188
Fetal Rh D genotype assessment from maternal blood can eliminate administration of anti-D immune globulin in such cases, thereby reducing costs and potential risk.

770
01:43:22,188 --> 01:43:34,275
When Rh D alloimmunization is suspected, early identification of an Rh D-negative fetus might avoid unnecessary middle cerebral artery Doppler assessment or amniocentesis.

771
01:43:34,275 --> 01:43:46,125
Evaluation using cell-free DNA is done using real-time PCR to target several exons of the RHD gene, typically exons 4, 5, 7, and 10.

772
01:43:46,125 --> 01:43:59,500
Rh D-genotyping is performed routinely with cell-free DNA in Denmark, Finland, and the Netherlands (Clausen, 2012; de Haas, 2016; Haimila, 2017).

773
01:43:59,500 --> 01:44:15,000
In population-based studies of more than 35,000 Rh D-negative women screened at 24 to 27 weeks’ gestation, the false-negative rate—in which Rh D-negative status was missed—was only 0.03 percent.

774
01:44:15,000 --> 01:44:26,450
The falsepositive rate—in which Rh immune globulin would be given unnecessarily— was less than 1 percent (de Haas, 2016; Haimila, 2017).

775
01:44:26,450 --> 01:44:33,625
Similar results were reported from the United Kingdom, although the false-negative rate was higher in the first trimester.

776
01:44:33,625 --> 01:44:42,513
Investigators concluded that false-negative screening results might increase the alloimmunization risk but by less than 1 case per million births .

777
01:44:42,513 --> 01:44:48,538
Rh D alloimmunization is discussed in Chapter 18 (p. 353).

